window.MT2FW_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title": "Build a mod with an agent", "href": "agents/start.html", "text": " # Build a mod with an agent Use the same public contract and complete examples as a human modder. The current Lua API is **1.0.0**; the framework is **0.2.0** and native C ABI is **1**. The [compatibility page](../compatibility.md) identifies the supported game build. ## Context to give your agent Read this page, the [quickstart](../quickstart.md), [API reference](../reference/index.md) and [machine contract](../public/api/1.0.0/api.json). A generated project includes `AGENTS.md`, `.mt2fw/mt2.lua` and `.mt2fw/api.json` for offline use. Source examples are under `examples/` in the SDK documentation bundle and repository. Use `.md` counterparts of the HTML pages for compact reading, or follow [llms.txt](../public/llms.txt). Discovery by an agent is not automatic; provide the entry file or link explicitly. Documentation exports include a versioned Markdown copy under `versions/1.0.0/`. Experimental typed patches require Framework 0.2.0 or newer. Read the [patch guide](../experimental/patches.md) and [patch target contract](../public/api/experimental/patches.json) before using them. Never infer Host authority from a manifest or a missing session flag. ## Author and validate ```powershell mt2fw new yourname.hello C:/Mods/yourname.hello --template hello mt2fw check C:/Mods/yourname.hello mt2fw pack C:/Mods/yourname.hello C:/Mods/hello-1.0.0.zip ``` Edit only the generated mod. Keep its namespace and declare capabilities. Register windows once, keep widget calls inside render callbacks, and wait for `world_ready` before world-dependent work. `start` is still preparation. The first Hello template has a `local message` line that can be changed directly. To add a setting, define it with `mt2.config.define` and read it inside your window. Use the complete `examples/config` project and the [function contract](../reference/functions/config-define.md). Lua needs no compiler. ## Rules that prevent damaging mistakes - Preserve exact handle UIDs as strings. World epochs expire. Persist supported building references with world storage and confirmed saves. - Accepted actions are queued. Only confirmed is success. Preserve unknown outcomes and inspect state; never auto-retry a mutation. - Lua reload replaces registrations but does not undo world changes. Content/native changes need restart. - Use public APIs only. Raw addresses, arbitrary process starts and native Lua modules are outside the regular Lua contract. - Keep source/package checks distinct from real game acceptance. A synthetic host can prove syntax and API wiring, not native world effects. ## Verify the result Check the generated manifest, run `check`, pack outside the source folder, and retain output and package hash. Then load the mod in an owned profile, edit the greeting, reload and inspect the visible result. Ask the human for genuinely missing intent, not for routine reversible authoring choices. Framework contributors should also read repository `AGENTS.md` and [contributor build guide](../contributing/build.md). Human newcomer timing and multiplayer bridge acceptance remain separate release gates. "}, {"title": "Compatibility and status", "href": "compatibility.html", "text": " # Compatibility and status This is a public development preview: framework **0.2.0**, Lua API **1.0.0**, native ABI **1**. It supports only Windows x64 with Steam and the exact verified MT2 executable: ```text bbab2dc68babcc644d0a1679548bf1e334b6c52aea669c3f61f6cb9d45c1aaf6 ``` Unsupported game builds leave the adapter and mods disabled and report the cause. A signature match alone never establishes compatibility. The installer preserves unknown loaders and original game archives. The runtime does not resolve internal game functions through the executable's symbol table. The adapter uses addresses and byte checks for the verified build. Symbols have helped with reverse engineering, so removing them makes future adapter work harder. A changed executable, including one with only its symbol table removed, still needs compatibility checks and an adapter update before support is enabled. ## Accepted behavior Retained private-game checks cover independent Lua/native plugins, shared hooks, transactional Lua replacement, callback failure isolation, camera/time controls, native place/move/remove operations, stale handles, two authored GLB packs, persistent building identity and missing-content load rejection. Portable installation, update, ZIP import and removal were checked on a separate game copy, including Unicode profile/world names. The empty-core p95 measured 0.3478 ms over 2048 samples in the retained paused fixture. The baseline includes the measurement probe and diagnostics, so this is not a pure uninstrumented-game benchmark. Candidate hashes remain in the acceptance record. New source/build checks do not retroactively change those historical hashes. ## Current limits - Lua is primary. C# integration is not part of this candidate. - GLB conversion is static scenery; animation and skeletons are unsupported. - Selected objects expose verified public handle/position information, not arbitrary native properties. - Root-world building references can persist; unsupported targets are rejected. - Native plugins are not unloaded during a session. Native memory faults can terminate the game. - Human newcomer timing is pending. Automated onboarding does not establish a human ten-minute claim. - Multiplayer bridge F with two separate Steam accounts is pending. Shared writes and multiplayer compatibility are not advertised. ## Experimental patches in 0.2.0 The [patch API](experimental/patches.md) shares ordering between Lua and native plugins. It observes the existing native Update and object-create envelopes. `api.time.set` mediates Framework requests only; it does not intercept native game clock controls. An isolated game run confirmed real Update pairs, a shared Lua/native create observation with a confirmed placed object, a Framework speed request limited from 4 to 2, rejected and successful Lua replacement, owner-only callback failure and world-epoch change. Exact binary identities and scope are recorded in [patch acceptance](public/evidence/patches-acceptance.json). Session-provider roles and retirement boundaries also have controlled native fixture coverage; this does not establish two-account multiplayer compatibility. `mt2.time.set` now requires acknowledged standalone or trusted Host authority. An active supported Multiplayer plugin without a session provider has unknown authority and cannot use this operation. Clients are denied. The general multiplayer bridge fence still applies, including for a provider reporting Host. ## Reload and migration Lua script reload stages a replacement and commits registrations at frame end. Failed replacements keep the old instance. Already executed world actions remain. Content and native plugin updates require restart. Stable API changes stay compatible within the documented API major version. Check [generated changes](reference/changes.md), the versioned contract and migration notes before updating. A function signature check cannot establish unchanged behavior by itself. "}, {"title": "Build and contribute", "href": "contributing/build.html", "text": " # Build and contribute Use Windows x64, Visual Studio 2022 Desktop development with C++, CMake 3.24 or newer, Python 3.11 or newer, and Node 22 or newer for documentation. The repository vendors native dependencies, including pinned Lua 5.4.9 source, and needs no multiplayer checkout or installed game to compile and run core/example checks. ```powershell python tools/build.py python -m unittest discover -s tests -p \"test_*.py\" python tools/check_contract.py ``` Builds and detailed logs stay under ignored `build/` and `artifacts/`. `--core-only` omits the Windows adapter. `--skip-tests` is available when the unchanged core scope has already passed and the affected scope is checked separately. ## Build documentation ```powershell npm ci --ignore-scripts python tools/generate_docs.py npm run docs:build npm run docs:test ``` The API contract generates the Lua SDK, public function/event/type pages, schemas and change report. The native ABI page includes the authoritative header declarations. Examples are included from real project files. `--check` reports stale outputs without rewriting them. The documentation build produces `dist/docs/site/`, `dist/docs/offline/` and `dist/docs/markdown/`. Open the offline `index.html` directly. All images, search data and code copying work without a server or CDN. Browser tests use Playwright Chromium; set `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE` to reuse an installed browser. ## Build portable tools and a local product ```powershell python tools/build_helper.py --bootstrap build/helper-env/Scripts/python.exe tools/package_product.py ``` Build documentation first. The helper embeds the same generated SDK, contract, agent guide and offline documentation. The package builder includes only owned runtime files, SDK/templates, examples and notices. It never installs into the real game or publishes the result. ## Source map `src/core` owns plugin/runtime services. `src/loader` owns bootstrap and forwarding. `adapters/mt2` owns game layouts and exact-build guards; its internal extracted namespace is not public SDK. `third_party` has pinned native sources and original notices. `licenses` records origin and file hashes. Do not distribute original game binaries, archives, private profiles or disassembly. Preserve the original multiplayer project's license and data. Real-game acceptance requires an owned process and separate profile; evidence from documentation checks must not be described as game acceptance. "}, {"title": "Native app entry and New Game handoff", "href": "experimental/native-app-integration.html", "text": " # Native app entry and New Game handoff Requires Framework Runtime 0.1.6 or newer. Lua API 1.0.0 and native C ABI 1 remain unchanged. These MT2-only JSON operations are experimental. They use the existing native host request bridge, require the `ui` capability and belong in a render callback on the bound game thread. The stable Lua API and native plugin C ABI are unchanged. ## Optional Desktop entry Register the same app before drawing its native window: ```json { \"id\": \"main\", \"title\": \"Multiplayer\", \"desktop\": true, \"icon\": \"assets/multiplayer.png\" } ``` Send this object to `ui.app`. The host supplies the caller's mod identity. `desktop` defaults to false, so existing registrations keep their central Mods launcher behavior. One app is registered per mod. The session supports at most 64 mod Desktop entries. `icon` is optional. It names a PNG under the caller's own package `assets/` directory, or uses the existing data-directory `cache/ .png` contract. Cache file contents must match the filename hash. Both forms fit within 1 MiB and have dimensions no larger than 2048 x 2048. The Framework reads and caches the image once, without file operations per frame. Changing image contents needs a restart. Without an icon, the entry uses the Framework's Mods artwork. The existing native Desktop owns the icon and its dragging. A double-click queues only that app's `open_requested` flag. Repeated input before the next callback coalesces into one request. `ui.app` consumes that flag once, just as the central Mods launcher does. A mod must not treat registration as an instruction to open its window every frame. The response adds a `desktop` object: | Field | Meaning | | --- | --- | | `requested` | The caller requested Desktop exposure. | | `registered` | An icon for this app is confirmed on the current native Desktop. | | `visible` | Observed visibility, or null when it is unknown. | | `visibility_known` | Whether `visible` has a confirmed value. | | `error` | Original registration or presentation failure, otherwise an empty string. | Registration may complete on the next Framework frame. Entries are shown only on the Title Desktop. The in-game `Mods...` menu and the central launcher remain available. An inactive owner loses pending open requests and its icon is hidden; its native icon storage remains owned by the Desktop until native destruction. A failed icon does not disable another app or the central Mods window. Native icon creation with an unknown result is not automatically repeated. ## Open the original New Game wizard After registering the app, send an empty object to `ui.new_world` in response to the user's New World action. This opens the original MT2 wizard. It does not create a world or select any settings. Only `confirmed: true` with `status` of `opened` or `already_open` permits the caller to hide or suspend its own app window: ```json { \"status\": \"already_open\", \"confirmed\": true, \"attempted\": false, \"visible\": true, \"mode\": 2, \"window\": \"NewGame\", \"route\": \"mmoModeTitle::ShowCreateWindow\" } ``` The successful states are `already_open` and `opened`. An already visible wizard is acknowledged without calling the launcher, preserving its edited name, seed and page. A hidden wizard uses the original launcher once, then verifies the same owner, registry entry, class and visible state. Failure returns `confirmed: false`, `status` of `rejected` or `unknown`, an `attempted` flag and the original `cause`. A rejected request made no native launch call. An unknown result must not trigger another launch: a later observation can acknowledge the now-visible wizard, otherwise the operation remains blocked for that Title epoch. A native mode change retires the old attempt. The caller retains its lobby state. If the player cancels the native wizard, it can reopen its registered app. If the player uses the original Create control, Multiplayer's existing world-readiness and Host-adoption path remains responsible for shared preparation. This API does not acknowledge that later world creation or multiplayer admission. To detect cancellation, send `{\"observe\":true}` to the same operation. This performs only the ownership and visibility reads, never the launcher. Its success receipt has `status: \"observed\"`, `confirmed: true`, `attempted: false` and the actual `visible` value. A cancelled hidden wizard stays hidden. `confirmed` here confirms only that observation. After Title is left, this read mode rejects with `attempted: false`; the caller's existing world-readiness path handles the new world. "}, {"title": "Native document UI (experimental)", "href": "experimental/native-document-ui.html", "text": " # Native document UI (experimental) Framework 0.1.4 adds a central app launcher, native game windows and an optional document renderer to the native host JSON interface. These experimental operations are separate from stable Lua API 1.0.0. The C ABI stays at version 1. The [machine contract](../public/api/experimental/native-document-ui.json) defines the accepted fields and limits. Call these operations from a `render` callback with the `ui` capability. The host supplies the calling mod's identity. Never supply another mod's identity. Networking and PNG decoding belong in a worker, not in the render callback. ## Central entry and native windows The in-game bottom-right start menu contains **Mods...** before **Options...**. It opens the same native Mods window as the desktop icon and optional framework F10 shortcut. Mods do not need separate function keys. Call `ui.app` every render callback, including while your app is closed: ```json {\"id\":\"main\",\"title\":\"My mod\"} ``` This registers one app for the calling active mod instance. The selected mod receives an **Open My mod** button. The response `{\"open_requested\":true}` consumes that app's launch request. It is never sent to another mod. A replacement instance registers again; inactive instances cannot launch. Keep the app ID and title stable. Call `ui.native` while open to display an actual game-owned window: ```json { \"id\":\"main\",\"open\":true,\"width\":600,\"height\":400, \"widgets\":[ {\"id\":\"search\",\"type\":\"input\",\"x\":20,\"y\":24,\"width\":400,\"height\":34,\"value\":\"\"}, {\"id\":\"import\",\"type\":\"button\",\"x\":20,\"y\":80,\"width\":180,\"height\":34,\"text\":\"Import card\"} ] } ``` The response contains `ready`, `open`, `clicked` and `values`. Wait if `ready` is false during a mode transition. Store input changes from `values`, process `clicked` once, and stop submitting after the user closes the window. To reopen, wait for the next launcher request. The native close button, window placement, text fields, button activation and Escape handling belong to MT2. Framework management temporarily hides the app window and restores it when closed. Native `input` widgets accept `max_bytes`, an integer from 1 to 1024 with default 128. Chat can declare `\"max_bytes\":240`. This field is part of the immutable window layout, so changing it requires a new native mod instance. Scripted values and diagnostic text must fit the same UTF-8 byte limit. Native editing clips only at complete UTF-8 boundaries and confirms the pane's readback; it does not split a multibyte character. Diagnostic setters return byte count and matching-readback acknowledgement without logging the text. This limit is separate from the document renderer's input budget below. `open` preserves the existing desired/open state while Framework management suspends the app. `visible` reports the actual native window visibility flag, with `visibility_known` true after readback. It is null if the current native window is unavailable. `suspended` reports the Framework management state and is null while the mode is unavailable. A native visible flag does not prove focus, window order or unobscured rendered pixels. Retain actual action acknowledgement and screenshots for those claims. The window uses native MT2 blue panel materials. Buttons support style button (primary action), tab, row and quiet. Panels use tone subtle for recessed content and preview for the main reading area. Text supports muted and error tones. These are bounded native styles, not arbitrary resource paths. Layout is fixed for a mod instance; changes to positions, widget types, sizes or IDs require a restart. Change text, values, visibility, disabled and selected states without rebuilding the layout. All widgets stay inside the window. The machine contract lists supported fields and budgets. No filesystem read or layout-file write occurs on unchanged frames. Native images use immutable original PNG files in the mod-owned `cache/ .png` directory. The host checks the filename hash, PNG signature, dimensions and size, then creates a namespaced material. Previews are limited to 1 MiB, 2048 pixels per dimension and 128 unique images per session. Game-owned materials remain loaded for the session. Keep card transfer and image decoding outside render callbacks. The existing document renderer below remains available for developer tools. ## Documents Submit a complete document each frame. Keep document and widget IDs stable. Containers scope their child IDs. The renderer owns the window, styles and balanced ImGui scopes, including error paths. ```json { \"document\": { \"id\": \"browser\", \"title\": \"My card browser\", \"width\": 1120, \"height\": 660, \"content\": { \"type\": \"panel\", \"id\": \"main\", \"children\": [ {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Community cards\", \"tone\": \"heading\"}, {\"type\": \"input\", \"id\": \"search\", \"hint\": \"Search names...\", \"value\": \"\"}, {\"type\": \"button\", \"id\": \"import\", \"text\": \"Import card\", \"primary\": true} ] } } } ``` The result is `{\"clicked\": [\"import\"], \"values\": {\"search\": \"Dragon\"}, \"open\": true}`. `clicked` and `values` contain only changes in this frame. Store input values in the mod. Stop submitting a document when `open` becomes false. Escape closes the focused document. | Widget | Fields | | --- | --- | | `text` | `text`, optional `tone`: `heading`, `muted`, `success`, `error` | | `button` | `id`, `text`, optional `primary`, `selected`, `disabled`, `width` | | `input` | `id`, `value`, `hint`, optional `disabled`; maximum 256 UTF-8 bytes | | `selectable` | `id`, `title`, `subtitle`, optional `selected`, `disabled` | | `image` | `image` cache ID, optional maximum `width`, `height`; aspect ratio preserved | | `panel` | `id`, `children`, optional `height` for a scrollable region | | `columns` | `children`; child `width` fixes a column, zero uses remaining space | | `separator` | No extra fields | | `space` | Optional `height` | Limits: 200 widgets, depth 8, four columns per container, 2048 bytes per widget string and 4096 pixels per widget dimension. Invalid definitions return a host error before rendering begins. Window title and identity are limited to 128 bytes. Document windows initially fit the game viewport and can be resized. ## Images Write an immutable preview file under the calling mod's own `data/ /` directory. Its binary format is the eight bytes `M2RGBA1\\0`, little-endian unsigned 32-bit width and height, then tightly packed RGBA8 pixels. Dimensions must be 1-2048 pixels, with exactly `16 + width * height * 4` bytes. Keep the original PNG separate and unchanged. Call `ui.image.load` with `{\"id\": \" \", \"path\": \"cache/ .rgba\"}`. Paths must be relative to this mod's data directory. Parent traversal, absolute paths and reparse points are rejected. A confirmed result is `{\"id\": \" \", \"ready\": true}`. This first call reads and uploads the bounded file. Repeated calls with the same ID reuse the texture. Use a new content hash when pixels change. Each mod retains up to 16 textures. Least recently used textures are evicted. Inactive owners and textures unused for 1800 render frames are collected on the render thread. Texture upload restores the game's previous OpenGL unpack and texture bindings. Keyboard events captured by a document stay in the framework. Text entry suppresses letter hotkeys while function-key bindings remain available. This is shared framework behavior, so other native mods can reuse it. ## Open apps in the game ![Mods... before Options...](../public/media/mods-menu-entry.png) ![Open Card Vault from Mods](../public/media/mod-apps.png) "}, {"title": "Retained originals and typed imports", "href": "experimental/native-ownership.html", "text": " # Retained originals and typed imports Framework 0.1.5 provides experimental retained-hook and typed-import ownership through `MT2FW_MT2_QueryOwnership`. Its declarations are in `adapters/mt2/sdk/native_ownership.h` and the developer package's `sdk/native/mt2` directory. ServicesV1, RoutesV1 and stable plugin ABI 1 keep their existing declarations. Query from an active Framework callback with the exact borrowed host pointer. ## Disable without freeing originals `hook_disable` restores the authenticated original entry while retaining the actual trampoline, hook owner and native receiver state. It accepts a prepared or already disabled owned target without another mutation. `hook_resume` enables the same hook and trampoline again. The private state is `DISABLED_RETAINED` (5). Shared Framework targets and other owners reject. This is required by the current Multiplayer native-free retirement, Move failure and published building-history failure paths. Mapping those calls to remove would free storage still used by late entries. Retention stays until process exit, including after resume. `hook_remove` returns busy for retained routes. Fresh unpublished ordinary prepared hooks remain removable; the sized native-free target is sticky from preparation, matching its original reclamation prohibition. The host emits the normal mod `stop` event before retiring physical routes. A mod must use this event to suspend sessions, transport and workers while preserving published native receiver storage. For retained native state, PluginShutdown and host/context destruction wait until process exit. Expected retention is reported as info. A changed foreign route, unconfirmed restoration or in-flight callback remains a failed retirement and retains its original cause. It is not reported as completed cleanup. Retained managers and host contexts live in process-owned storage without a static destructor. DLL teardown cannot destroy a manager before the late native entries that still own its originals. A repeated shutdown does not repeat an unknown reversal, and retained state cannot be replaced by another initialization in the same process. ## Exact free import ownership The service only controls the verified executable's `msvcrt.dll!free` slot at RVA `0x1c52a00`, with `malloc` at `0x1c52a70` as a sibling guard. There is no caller-selected DLL, symbol, address or slot. The backend checks the named PE import and the exact game hash. It pins the imported CRT and actual resolved free/malloc provider modules, holds their files against replacement, and reports paths, SHA256, file sizes, fixed version presence and export forwarders. Missing fixed version metadata is explicit, not a fabricated version. Provider file reads occur during preparation, never per free call. 1. Call `free_prepare` with the existing native free observer entry. Require the exact original/provider/sibling-slot receipt. 2. Publish the current Multiplayer observer disabled with the sized trampoline and `receipt.original`. Keep its existing body, world, thread and ownership guards. 3. Enable the sized hook, then call `free_enable` and require exchanged pointer, restored initial protection and live route acknowledgement before enabling the observer. 4. Suspend and quiesce the existing observer. Call `free_disable` and retaining `hook_disable` for the sized route. Validate actual original pointers, protection and retained originals. 5. `free_remove` can retire import metadata only while prepared or confirmed disabled. It cannot remove an enabled or unknown import or free pinned provider modules. The IAT points directly to the existing Multiplayer observer. No extra per-free dispatcher changes the original caller address. The service does not reimplement the accepted delete/reclamation policy or execute a free during control operations. Every pointer exchange is one aligned compare-and-exchange against an expected measured pointer. A competing foreign pointer remains untouched. Receipts separate attempted, exchanged, protection restored, live confirmed, Win32/SEH errors and original cause. `protection_restored` is meaningful for an attempted exchange; an untouched prepared import does not claim a restoration occurred. An exchange that never started remains distinguishable from an unknown mutation. Unknown enable cannot be replayed. Readback does not change state or erase the original cause. Explicit cleanup first authenticates the current provider, sibling slot and actual pointer. It can restore an acknowledged owned replacement or repair initial protection after a measured prior restoration failure. It refuses a foreign pointer. The Framework owns protection changes; the plugin validates the receipt instead of repeating raw VirtualProtect writes. ## Passive Mode.Command observation `command_subscribe` observes the actual gameplay Mode.Command entry before Framework dispatch. Each borrowed record has invocation, receiver, thread, exact bounded text and a readable/error status. It can count the original `LevelUp` callback without installing another title/game command hook. There is no argument replacement, continuation or suppression result. Callback failure is retained while the original dispatch still proceeds normally. Use the existing `ingame.command` route/code receipt for physical owner admission. ## Accepted SDL events `input_subscribe` observes only events accepted by the single Framework SDL_PollEvent provider, after its reserved-key and UI-consumption checks. It includes unbound key-down, key-up and other forwarded events. It cannot filter input or install another event pump. The record supplies a borrowed SDL_Event copy, byte size, compiled SDL version, thread and invocation. Validate size/version before decoding. The event structure is copied independently for each observer; pointer payloads remain borrowed and must be copied if retained. Observer failure cannot suppress the event returned to the game or change another mod's event structure. Null polls and consumed events are not emitted. This does not claim that an injected event produced a native game effect; that still requires acknowledgement or visible readback. All observers belong to the mod. A busy unsubscribe retains callback data until its invocation returns and stops future calls. Automatic retirement uses the same lifetime rule. These services remain private native contracts and do not isolate memory faults. ## Acceptance Synthetic tests cover retaining disable/resume, original-pointer lifetime, exact-provider admission, owned/foreign CAS, protection failure and explicit recovery, never-executed versus unknown operations, metadata retirement, passive command/input subscriptions and callback lifetime. Native input byte-limit checks cover the unchanged default, Chat's 240-byte boundary and invalid/multibyte UTF-8. These fixtures do not prove physical input or arbitrary native plugin behavior. Retained game tests cover the specific migrated action cycle, initial-save metadata, Profiler measurements and window lifecycle; full Multiplayer compatibility is not established. "}, {"title": "Native shared routes and hook ownership", "href": "experimental/native-routes.html", "text": " # Native shared routes and hook ownership Framework 0.1.5 provides experimental shared routes through `MT2FW_MT2_QueryRoutes`, version 1, with the exact host pointer from PluginLoad. Declarations are in `adapters/mt2/sdk/native_routes.h` and the developer package's `sdk/native/mt2` directory. Stable plugin ABI 1 remains unchanged. ## Named catalog and phased hooks `adapters/mt2/multiplayer-catalog.json` maps 130 unique EXE targets to stable private names, source groups and installation phases from the sealed Multiplayer inventory. Eleven existing EXE hooks remain Framework-owned. Catalog admission does not install or enable any Multiplayer hook. Existing initial, world-ready and lazy owner guards still apply. Dormant pattern scanners, debug-register probes and exception traps are excluded. 1. Query the route service in an active Framework callback. 2. Call `hook_prepare` with a catalog name and your detour. Require `OK`, `PREPARED`, the expected owner/entry and a nonzero actual trampoline. 3. Publish your existing disabled receiver with that trampoline, retaining your current body, thread and owner checks. 4. Call `hook_enable`. Require `OK` and `ENABLED`, then validate the owned route receipt. 5. Retire only after the feature's own quiescence and reclamation guards permit it. Use `hook_remove` for the exact owner and named target. The Framework owns MinHook, individual target activation and rollback. Plugins never initialize or uninitialize it and never use enable-all or disable-all. A known failed `MH_CreateHook` does not acquire another owner's slot. An unknown operation or changed live route cannot be automatically replayed or blindly overwritten. Receipts retain the failed condition and actual state. DLLs, originals and native detour storage remain session-owned; there is no general hot-unload claim. Each receipt includes executable hash, RVA, entry, trampoline, detour, generation, owner, acknowledged pristine/current prefixes, state and original cause. The Windows backend reads the exact executable once, requires its supported SHA256, maps executable PE sections and checks live bytes. Enabled paths must also resolve through a bounded jump proof to the acknowledged detour. A receipt is a current observation, not permission to ignore later changes. ## Original code admission Keep existing native guard expectations. For a patched named target, call `code_verify` with its target-relative offset and the original expected bytes. This checks the expected bytes against the verified executable, the acknowledged current patch and unchanged live body bytes outside the patch prefix. Windows, DLL and address values stay in the private adapter contract. In particular, Move at `0x55ed80` has no Multiplayer detour but is observed by the Framework. A successful `objects.move` receipt plus original-body guard admission replaces the assumption that its entry must be unpatched. Native Move calls still use the receipt's entry and traverse the observer exactly once. Do not call its trampoline to bypass observation. The same receipt mechanism covers Framework Update, title/game/save commands and desktop/StartMenu routes that the older resolver checked as pristine. ## CreateBuilding `create_subscribe` supplies typed before/after observations of one unchanged original call. It permits no suppression or argument rewrite. The after callback receives the exact returned pointer and whether original returned normally. The adapter retains and rethrows original C++ exceptions after the after observations. Each invocation has its own ID, so nested calls do not share mutable per-call identity. The underlying Framework prop observer stays inside the original continuation. Multiplayer retains its own scoped attempted/returned/result observation. Purchase and update suppression stays in the unique upstream routes. Callback errors are logged on the game thread without inventing an original result or executing another original. ## DestroyProps authority `remove_bind` registers one authority owner. The adapter's noinline native entry captures the original return-slot address, immediate return address and thread before calling the dispatcher. The borrowed slot still refers to the native caller's frame; it is not a frame inside the added dispatcher. The Multiplayer adapter can reuse its exact caller-frame classification and existing capture/replay policy. The authority receives a typed per-invocation continuation. It retains the original manager/array and rejects a second attempt. Return `SUPPRESSED` only when no original was attempted, `FORWARDED` only after one normal original return, or `UNKNOWN` when the result is uncertain. Unknown, callback failure and inconsistent dispositions never trigger an automatic original. The original C++ exception is retained internally, reported as a failed continuation, then rethrown after the authority returns. Workers bypass game-thread authority and forward unchanged once. Unowned routes also forward once. On the game thread, foreign/standalone/empty/authorized-replay decisions remain in the existing Multiplayer authority code. Owned UI rejection remains suppressed. The Framework does not recreate that policy from the number of selected objects. Suspend the Multiplayer authority/session before unbinding. A busy unsubscribe/retirement means an invocation still owns the callback/user data. The owner must retain that state until confirmed quiescence. Framework shutdown keeps the runtime if a hook or shared invocation cannot retire. It does not report cleanup success while a route remains live. ## Acceptance and limits The separate [ownership extension](native-ownership.md) supplies typed `msvcrt!free` import control, retained disable/resume and passive Mode/input observations. ServicesV1 and RoutesV1 remain separately versioned contracts. Plugins must preserve their native free/delete/reclamation policy when adopting shared routes. Retained game tests cover the migrated place/move/remove action cycle; they do not establish full Multiplayer compatibility or support for arbitrary native hooks. Local tests exercise Create original-once/result/exception, Remove suppression/continuation/provenance/worker/failure paths, named ownership, prepare/enable ordering, guard admission, foreign patch rejection, failed creation isolation and unknown outcomes. They use synthetic memory and functions. The Windows build confirms compilation; it does not prove caller provenance or gameplay effects in the actual game. "}, {"title": "Native MT2 services", "href": "experimental/native-services.html", "text": " # Native MT2 services Framework 0.1.5 provides experimental MT2 native services alongside stable plugin ABI 1. The declarations are in `adapters/mt2/sdk/native_services.h`, and the developer package includes them under `sdk/native/mt2`. Keep these versioned game services separate from the stable Lua and native contracts. ## Bootstrap and ownership Use the regular `MT2FW_PluginQuery`, `MT2FW_PluginLoad` and `MT2FW_PluginShutdown` exports. Keep the exact borrowed host pointer received at Load. Load is preparation; discover the service in the first active callback by resolving `MT2FW_MT2_QueryService` on the already loaded `MT2Framework.dll`. Pass service version 1 and the full output size. An absent export or unavailable result means the required experimental service is unavailable. It never authorizes another loader. The framework validates the actual host identity and supplies trusted game, profile, isolated game-data and mod-owned data paths. `game_data_directory` is the trusted profile container, not necessarily the active write root: PHYSFS may use its Steam-account child. Resolve the acknowledged write directory rather than assuming both paths are identical. All service registration, hook and capture-control calls belong in framework callbacks. Scope emission, capture draining and section lookup can run on workers. Workers must stop before plugin shutdown returns. A retired interface rejects new registrations; keep all native code and detour storage alive for the session. These services do not isolate native memory faults. ## Paired update callbacks `frame_subscribe` registers both callbacks as one owned pair. A begin result of 1 enters the pair; 0 skips it. Each entered begin receives one end, in reverse subscription order. The frame contains the game receiver, a monotonic frame ID and delta time. End indicates whether original Update returned normally. The framework invokes original Update once between these phases. Existing ordinary `update` events remain after this pair. Use the pair to preserve a simulation fence that must surround original Update. The after callback can retain the existing Multiplayer order: replay, transport, time, HUD, diagnostics, close the fence, then lobby preparation. Do not move a fence into the ordinary post-update event. A plugin retired during the original call still receives its entered end. Its native shutdown waits for this callback lease to close. Shutdown requested during a frame is deferred until the pair completes. Callbacks must return promptly and must not throw across the native boundary. A throwing begin violates the contract and cannot promise cleanup of resources it acquired before throwing. Native teardown must not destroy detour state while an engine invocation is still using it. ## Hook catalog The framework owns MinHook initialization, named target ownership, enable/disable and rollback. A native plugin can request a named adapter target; it cannot supply a target RVA or executable address. Different names cannot alias the same catalogued address. A claimed target cannot be claimed by another plugin. Failed installation and failed rollback retain ownership until actual cleanup succeeds. The current candidate reserves existing framework targets, including original Update, title/game commands, native desktop and StartMenu, create/move/remove observers, SDL and PhysFS. It deliberately has no live unique Multiplayer catalog entries yet. The Multiplayer hook inventory must provide exact verified targets and installation phases first. Shared operations require typed adapter dispatch and an owned original-call receipt; they cannot use a second detour or blind chaining. This remains an unfinished migration slice. The loader continues to reject the legacy `MT2_Multiplayer.dll`. The migrated plugin uses `MT2MultiplayerPlugin.dll`. It must not call the standalone bootstrap, install another profile redirect, initialize another renderer/input system, call `MH_ALL_HOOKS` or uninitialize the process hook manager. ## Optional profiling feed Register immutable named sections once. Cache the service and section IDs. `scope_begin` returns `{epoch, token}`; always pass the same section/token to `scope_end`. With no capture active, begin returns zero before reading a clock or allocating memory. No per-scope JSON, file access or observer callback runs. Only one plugin owns a capture at a time. `capture_start` preallocates 64 to 1,048,576 fixed 56-byte event slots. Producers use a bounded try-lock write and count overflow or contention as dropped events. The capture owner drains batches with `capture_read`, then stops and drains remaining records. Stop preserves the queue. Starting another capture explicitly replaces the previous queue and advances its epoch. Old scope tokens cannot close scopes in another epoch. Events contain capture epoch, sequence, monotonic nanoseconds, thread ID, token, game-update frame ID, section ID and kind. Frame markers surround original Update and native pairs; they do not represent GPU completion, rendering or the full ordinary update callback period. Section metadata supplies the mod ID and name. `dropped` is cumulative for the capture. Buffer overflow, writer contention and producer retirement can make an interval incomplete. The consumer must retain this state. It must not invent exact exclusive times, complete call trees or maxima for an incomplete interval. The profiler mod owns trees, aggregation, interval analysis, reports, comparisons, UI and exports. The framework contains only the transport buffer and markers. This feed reports elapsed wall time. `thread_cpu_available` is 0; coarse CPU sampling in a separate profiler must be labelled separately. ## Acceptance Automated checks cover ownership, frame ordering and retirement, capture identity, concurrent producers, overflow, epochs, hook rollback and isolation between plugins. Actual game captures confirm balanced native sections and acknowledged export. A bounded paused-world Multiplayer capture confirms instrumented scopes; it does not establish rendered FPS, native thread CPU, active simulation performance or public Multiplayer compatibility. "}, {"title": "Ordered patches", "href": "experimental/patches.html", "text": " # Ordered patches Use Lua to observe named native calls and apply a typed policy to Framework time requests. Requires **MT2 Mod Framework 0.2.0 or later** with **experimental patch protocol 1**, separate from stable Lua API **1.0.0** and native ABI **1**. Use the SDK that matches the runtime. The [machine contract](../public/api/experimental/patches.json) describes each target, phase, payload and permitted effect. `game.update` and `objects.create` surround real native calls. `api.time.set` surrounds only requests made through `mt2.time.set`. It does **not** intercept the game's clock controls or every native clock call. All three targets use the framework's existing hook or request path. Lua mods need no raw addresses, signatures, compiler or native DLL. ## Observe a native create Start with the complete SDK project `examples/patch-observer`. Its in-game window counts native create entries and returns after `world_ready`. Counts stay in memory; the example does not write a log on each frame or create. 1. Copy `examples/patch-observer` from the SDK to `C:/Mods/patch-observer`. 2. Run `mt2fw check C:/Mods/patch-observer`. 3. Run `mt2fw pack C:/Mods/patch-observer C:/Mods/patch-observer.zip`. 4. Install the ZIP with the portable helper, open the game and load a world. 5. Place an object and read the **Native create observer** window. <<< ../../examples/patch-observer/main.lua{lua} The complete project includes `mod.json`, editor definitions and its license. Its capabilities are `ui` and `world.read`; it is a local mod. A native create is not the same as a successful queued placement action. `original_returned` means the native call returned normally. `result_available` means the native result was non-null. It supplies no pointer or UID and does not replace the action API's `confirmed` result. Lua observes create calls on the bound game thread. Worker-thread calls are not queued to a later frame and presented as surrounding the original. Native plugins retain their typed callback contract. Payloads are copied, and no raw pointer is exposed to Lua. Invocation, generation, thread and epoch IDs are exact decimal strings. ## Register and discover Call `mt2.patches.add(spec, {before=..., after=...})` once during preparation. Supply at least one callback. `id` is unique within your mod; `target` is an exact catalog ID. `mode` defaults to `observe`, and priority defaults to zero. Observer callbacks return no value. Editing their copied table cannot modify the original call. `mt2.patches.targets()` lists supported targets. `mt2.patches.status()` exposes the published generation, order and unresolved references. Both are available during preparation. Remove your own registration with `mt2.patches.remove(id)`. `remove` returns true after the owner has no live patch-invocation leases. False means removal applies to future calls while a current pair still retains its callback references. Status includes each patch's latest error and a suppressed-error count. | Target | Source | Effects | Capability | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `game.update` | Native Update | Observe Before/After | `world.read` | | `objects.create` | Native creation | Observe Before/After | `world.read` | | `api.time.set` | Framework time request | Observe or typed paused/speed policy | `world.read` for observe; `time.write` for modify | Patch callbacks must not issue world writes. Modifiers return proposed typed values instead. Use ordinary events and user-triggered UI for world actions, with their declared capabilities and authority checks. These patch targets do not provide argument writes for native calls, return replacement, skip-original, arbitrary detours or transpilers. ## Two observers can coexist Copy the observer project to a second directory, change its manifest ID to `yourname.second`, and install both packages. Their IDs are independent. Two observers on `objects.create` are valid; sharing a target is not itself a conflict. An explicit `before` or `after` list contains fully qualified `manifest-id:patch-id` strings. For example, a second patch can specify `after = {\"example.patch-observer:count\"}`. This refers to a patch, not a dependency or physical hook address. Absent references remain dormant and visible. A required mod also belongs in `mod.json` dependencies. Explicit constraints take precedence. Ready patches use descending priority, then byte-ordinal manifest ID and patch ID. Before runs in that order; entered After callbacks run in reverse: ```text example.patch-observer:count Before yourname.second:count Before one original native call yourname.second:count After example.patch-observer:count After ``` Lua and native registrations through this patch service share this order. Existing native services retain their established outer envelopes; a constraint does not reorder another service's legacy subscriptions. Registration changes affect a later invocation, not a half-completed pair. ## Apply a Framework time policy The complete `examples/time-policy` project owns the typed `paused`/`speed` writer slot for `api.time.set`. Its Before callback returns a partial replacement, `speed`, clamped to 2. It leaves `paused` unchanged. Effective speed must remain finite and nonnegative; zero is allowed. <<< ../../examples/time-policy/main.lua{lua} Use the same check, pack and install steps with `time-policy` in place of `patch-observer`. Load a world, then press **Request speed 4 (limit 2)**. With acknowledged standalone authority, the returned clock shows speed 2. The visible result preserves a rejected authority cause. Requests happen only on a button click and are never retried automatically. This policy applies to Framework requests from mods. The game's native clock controls are outside its scope. A second modify patch on `api.time.set` is an actual conflict because the combined paused/speed slot has one writer. The framework rejects the candidate instead of silently choosing the last writer. Read-only observers may still coexist. ## Authority and failures A manifest declaration is not host authority. This version permits patch mutations only in a proven standalone session. Unknown sessions and clients cannot execute them. A trusted provider can acknowledge Host, but requests remain blocked because this version has no shared Host-action bridge. This feature makes no multiplayer compatibility claim. An inactive but still loaded, pinned or retained Multiplayer bridge also has unknown authority. Disabling its mod does not prove that its session has disconnected. The original rejection reason stays visible. Read the original error in Mods or Developer tools. Errors identify the owner, patch, target and failed condition. Unsupported native mutation, unknown target, invalid phase or type, an ordering cycle and an exclusive writer conflict reject the candidate. A cycle includes its closed path. A Lua callback error disables its owning mod and preserves the original cause. Other owners continue; observer failures cannot suppress or replay the original native call. | Error | Meaning | | --- | --- | | `PATCH_TARGET: unknown target objects.creat` | Choose an exact ID from target discovery. | | `PATCH_READ_ONLY: objects.create supports observe only` | This native target has no writable arguments. | | `PATCH_READ_ONLY: observe callback must return nil` | Remove the observer's return value. | | `PATCH_READ_ONLY: patch callbacks cannot call shared-world write APIs; modifiers return writable values` | Return the modifier's proposal or move the world request to an ordinary event or user action. | | `PATCH_VALUES: speed must be finite and nonnegative` | Return a supported typed speed value. | | `PATCH_WRITER_CONFLICT: api.time.set paused/speed owned by example.time-policy:limit-speed; rejected yourname.second:limit-speed` | Keep one writer; observers may coexist. | | `PATCH_AUTHORITY_UNKNOWN: no acknowledged session authority` | The request was blocked before execution; no authority provider has acknowledged the session. | | `Trusted Multiplayer provider reports Client; patch mutation denied` | This process is a client; it cannot execute the mutation. | | `Trusted Multiplayer provider reports unknown session authority` | The provider has not established a usable role. | | `Multiplayer bridge is active without an acknowledged session provider` | A bridge is present, but it has supplied no current authority acknowledgement. | | `Trusted Multiplayer provider acknowledged Host; shared Host-action bridge is unavailable, patch mutation denied` | The role is known, but this version cannot route the request through a shared Host action. | The session adapter can provide a more specific client, unknown-session or transition reason. Preserve that text. If a writer throws or returns invalid values, the request reports `PATCH_VALUES: api.time.set writer failed; original request was not executed`; the attributed callback log retains the original cause. An initially denied time request enters no patch callback. After Before has run, a rejected writer or changed authority reports `original_returned=false, outcome=\"rejected\"` to the other entered After callbacks. A confirmed adapter return supplies `outcome=\"confirmed\"` and its clock `result`; an adapter exception supplies `outcome=\"unknown\"`. A failed Before does not enter its own After. ## Use the native C++ SDK Start from the complete `examples/native-patches` project. Its manifest declares `world.read`, and its CMake project includes the matching bundled native headers. The plugin registers one typed create observer from an active update callback. It resolves the extension from the already loaded framework and passes the exact borrowed Host pointer received at Load. No alternative loader or game address is needed. <<< ../../examples/native-patches/plugin.cpp{cpp} After the first completed create, the next active update requests retirement. `BUSY` stops future selection but requires callback code and user state to stay valid until a later retirement acknowledgement is `OK`. A failure preserves state and stops automatic retries. This example keeps its state for the process lifetime, so failure also cannot free live callback storage. Receipts and first retirement-state changes go to the Windows debugger output. Framework-owned retirement runs before plugin shutdown; never unload your DLL or free callback state to force a `BUSY` result to disappear. Callback records are borrowed until return, and no C++ exception may cross the C boundary. ## Reload safely Lua reload stages the owner's entire replacement set. If preparation, typing, authority-independent registration validation or ordering fails, the previous Lua instance and published order stay active. **Active** and **Reload rejected** describe separate states. Fix the reported cause, then reload again. A successful reload replaces the old registrations and resets example counters. Native plugins require restart. Reload does not undo a confirmed world change. Preserve an unknown world-action outcome and inspect current state; never replay it automatically. "}, {"title": "Settings and storage", "href": "guides/config-storage.html", "text": " # Settings and storage Define settings once. The Mods menu shows the schema and saves each mod's values inside its profile. ```lua mt2.config.define(\"greeting\", { type = \"string\", default = \"Hello, MT2!\", label = \"Greeting\" }) mt2.ui.window(\"My greeting\", function() mt2.ui.text(mt2.config.get(\"greeting\")) end) ``` Declare `ui` for this window. Boolean, integer, number and string settings are supported. Numeric schemas can define `min` and `max`; setting a wrong type or an out-of-range value reports an error. ## Keep profile data `mt2.storage.get(key, default)` and `set(key, value)` use the current mod's profile data. Use JSON-compatible values. Each scope has a 1 MiB budget. Arbitrary filesystem APIs, processes and native Lua DLLs are outside the regular Lua contract. ```lua mt2.events.on(\"start\", function() local starts = mt2.storage.get(\"starts\", 0) mt2.storage.set(\"starts\", starts + 1) end) ``` ## Keep world data Use the `\"world\"` scope only while a world is loaded. Data is associated with the exact confirmed game save. A `saved` event means the original save and its framework metadata have been confirmed. ```lua mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local visits = mt2.storage.get(\"visits\", 0, \"world\") mt2.storage.set(\"visits\", visits + 1, \"world\") end) ``` For root-world buildings, store `mt2.objects.persist(handle)` in world data, then use `mt2.objects.resolve(id)` after loading. The framework validates saved identity before returning a new handle. A deleted or mismatched building is an error, not a reason to create a replacement automatically. See [config](../reference/functions/config-define.md), [storage](../reference/functions/storage-get.md), [types](../reference/types.md) and complete projects under `examples/config` and `examples/storage`. "}, {"title": "Events and input", "href": "guides/events-input.html", "text": " # Events and input Register event subscriptions once. They belong to your mod and are removed when the instance ends or is successfully replaced. ```lua local token = mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function(event) mt2.log.info(\"World: \" .. event.name) end) -- Later, if needed: mt2.events.off(token) ``` The `start` event runs during preparation, before game-dependent APIs are available. Use `world_ready` to initialize work that needs a world. `world_leave` invalidates its session handles. A mod enabled into an existing world also receives `world_ready`. ## Bind a shortcut Declare the `input` capability. Bind SDL scancode 66 for F9: ```lua mt2.input.bind(\"show_message\", 66, function(event) mt2.log.info(\"Pressed: \" .. event.name) end) ``` F10 belongs to Mods. Reserved or conflicting shortcuts report their owner. Input events go only to the owning mod, and mod shortcuts are suspended while the Mods menu is open. ## Keep callbacks bounded Lua callbacks have instruction and memory budgets. An unhandled callback error disables its owner with the original cause. Dependency failures are shown for affected dependents. A failed replacement keeps the old instance, but world actions already performed cannot be undone by code reload. Use update events for small scheduled work. Avoid world scans, file operations and repeated registrations on every frame. See [event payloads](../reference/events.md), [input.bind](../reference/functions/input-bind.md) and [ui.window](../reference/functions/ui-window.md). "}, {"title": "Profile a mod", "href": "guides/profiling.html", "text": " # Profile a mod [MT2 Profiler](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-Profiler) is an optional native mod. Install it separately from the Framework runtime. It opens from the central **Mods...** window and records locally. 1. Install MT2 Profiler and restart the game. 2. Open **Mods...**, select **MT2 Profiler**, then choose **Open**. 3. Choose **Start capture**, reproduce the scene, then choose **Stop capture**. 4. Choose **Export capture** and wait for the confirmed file path. Captures are stored under `data/doodesch.profiler/captures` in the active Framework profile. The optional Python report tool produces Markdown and CSV output for comparing runs. See the [Profiler guide](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-Profiler/blob/main/README.md) and [measurement reference](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-Profiler/blob/main/docs/measurements.md). ## Interpret a result Use the same world, game speed and workload in each comparison. Native recordings measure wall time in instrumented sections, rather than stack samples or rendered FPS. A mod must provide instrumentation for its internal sections to appear. The raw event prefix is bounded; check the reported drops and incomplete frames before using a report. The Profiler uses the experimental [MT2 native services](../experimental/native-services.md). Those services are versioned separately from the stable native plugin ABI. Installing the Profiler does not establish Multiplayer compatibility. "}, {"title": "Import static scenery", "href": "guides/scenery.html", "text": " # Import static scenery Bring an original static GLB into the native Scenery catalog. Start with the **Scenery Pack** template to learn the package layout, then use **Import GLB** for your own model. ![Two authored models, including a textured model, rendered by the game](../public/media/scenery.png) ## Preview and convert Choose **Import GLB** in the helper. Inspect geometry, materials, scale, orientation, bounds and placement origin. Adjust scale and export a content package into your profile's `mods` folder. Restart the game before using new or changed content. The importer supports static triangle geometry, node transformations, normals, UVs, vertex colors, base colors and embedded PNG/JPEG textures. It reports unsupported animation, skins, morphs, sparse accessors, compression or material extensions. Materials map to the game's diffuse model; metallic/roughness differences are reported. ## Generated files A package contains `mod.json`, `content.json` and namespaced `assets`. The converter writes a native ModelV1 `.vmb`, material and scenery variant, translations and an editable `.vmd`. The native building catalog loads the binary model; renaming a GLB or VMD to VMB does not convert it. Content IDs use the mod namespace, for example `yourname.scenery:pyramid`. Duplicate IDs and competing resource names are reported. Resources are registered additionally, without merging arbitrary game archives. ## Save and load The framework records required content versions and hashes with confirmed saves. A missing required pack is named before loading, while the save remains unchanged. Keep the pack installed for worlds using its objects. ![An authored scenery building restored after a full game restart](../public/media/restored.png) These real captures come from retained game acceptance. Original game artwork in the captures has separate rights. See [content.register](../reference/functions/content-register.md), [content schema](../reference/manifests.md) and the complete `examples/content` project. "}, {"title": "Install with Vortex", "href": "guides/vortex.html", "text": " # Install with Vortex The [MMORPG Tycoon 2 Vortex Extension](https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/2465) installs the framework and its mods through Vortex. Each Vortex profile keeps separate settings, mod data and worlds. ## Requirements - Windows x64 and Vortex 2.8.2 or newer. - The [supported Steam game build](../compatibility.md). - The player runtime ZIP from [Nexus](https://www.nexusmods.com/mmorpgtycoon2/mods/25) or [GitHub Releases](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-ModFramework/releases). - A staging folder on the same volume as the game. ## Install 1. Close the game. Remove an existing manual framework installation with its original helper. 2. Open **Extensions** in Vortex, drop in the extension ZIP and restart Vortex. 3. Manage **MMORPG Tycoon 2** and select the Steam installation. 4. Import the player runtime ZIP as a mod, enable it and deploy. The developer SDK is a separate GitHub download and stays outside the game folder. 5. Import and enable your mod ZIPs, deploy, then start the game through Steam. **F10** opens Mods. Vortex manages deployment and removal for this installation. Keep the game closed while switching profiles or changing deployment. Original game archives are not replaced. Unknown loaders or modified managed files produce a named conflict. ## Classic scenery The extension also recognizes model-only scenery archives, including the original [More Sceneries - Lite Mode](https://www.nexusmods.com/mmorpgtycoon2/mods/2) download by Cow. Download such packs from their authors; the extension does not bundle them. Supported archives have one named pack folder containing `.vmb` models under `scenery/festival`, `scenery/prop` or `scenery/table`. Import the original ZIP without adding `mod.json`. Conflicting resource names are reported before deployment. Sign in to Steam before the first classic deployment. Models are placed in the selected Vortex profile and Steam account's game-data directory. Restart the game after scenery changes, and keep the packs required by your worlds. Classic packs use native save references without the framework's missing-content metadata. Deployment, profile switching, disable and purge have been checked with the original More Sceneries Lite 1.3 ZIP. The engine loaded one representative model. Interactive catalog placement and saving/reloading a placed classic object remain unverified; see the [acceptance record](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-ModFramework/blob/main/evidence/vortex-scenery-acceptance.json). Legacy MT2 Mod Manager `manifest.json` merging and replacements for original game archives are not supported. Multiplayer compatibility is not confirmed. ## Remove Close the game, purge deployment and remove the managed packages through Vortex. Profile data and saves remain available. Remove the extension itself after purging, then restart Vortex. For development, keep source projects outside Vortex's managed folders. Check and pack a mod with the helper or CLI, then import that ZIP into Vortex. "}, {"title": "Objects and world actions", "href": "guides/world-actions.html", "text": " # Objects and world actions Read supported objects through handles. A handle's epoch identifies the active world; its UID is an exact string. Do not convert that string to a Lua number or retain a session handle across world changes. ```lua mt2.ui.window(\"World Inspector\", function() if mt2.world.status().loaded and mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"Selected UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) end end end) ``` Declare `ui`, `world.read` and `objects.read`. The inspector reads explicitly rather than scanning every frame. Object lists are bounded to 128 results. ## Submit an action World writes need `objects.write` and a `singleplayer` or `shared` manifest class. Shared writes are currently unavailable in multiplayer. Configure an owned, clear buildable position before placing anything. ```lua local pending -- Call from a user-triggered callback in a loaded world: local function place_my_scenery(x, y, z) pending = mt2.actions.place(\"yourname.scenery:pyramid\", x, y, z) end mt2.events.on(\"action_completed\", function(result) if pending and result.id == pending.id then mt2.log.info(result.status) if result.cause and result.cause ~= \"\" then mt2.log.warn(result.cause) end end end) ``` | Status | Meaning | | --- | --- | | `accepted` | Queued for the game thread; no success yet. | | `confirmed` | The native operation and its readback confirmed success. | | `rejected` | No confirmed operation; inspect the cause. | | `unknown` | A native attempt may have occurred; inspect current state before any further action. | Never automatically retry `unknown`, a timed-out action or an unacknowledged mutation. Requests have unique IDs and belong to their mod. Placement uses native terrain height; movement can settle to native ground height. Lua reload preserves already performed world changes. Move and remove use the same status contract. Native object observers report supported original placement/move/removal as `object_changed`; these notifications do not promise every internal property change in the game. See [actions](../reference/functions/actions-place.md), [persistent references](../reference/functions/objects-persist.md) and [compatibility](../compatibility.md). "}, {"title": "Make room for your ideas", "href": "index.html", "text": " Your next idea belongs in the game Make room for your ideas. Build mods for MMORPG Tycoon 2. Start with Lua, extend with C++, or bring your own scenery into the world. Create your first mod ↗ Explore the API → Windows x64 + Steam Inside MMORPG Tycoon 2 Desktop · F10 Real game capture: the native Mods desktop app. View the full capture ↗ 🛟 Need help or found a bug? Get support at support.doodesch.de/modframework . Choose what you want to build. One framework. Three ways in. { } Lua scripts ↗ Add UI, react to world events and reload your code while the game is open. A text editor is enough ++ C++ plugins ↗ Use native entry points through a versioned C ABI and the shared framework services. For native development ◇ GLB scenery ↗ Import static models and register your own scenery in the game's catalog. Bring something you made A small first step Your words. In your world. Start from Hello MT2. Change the greeting, select Reload, and see the new text in the game. Follow the quickstart → main.lua Lua mt2.ui.window ( \"Hello MT2\" , function() mt2.ui.text ( \"I made this.\" ) end) Register the window once. The callback draws its contents. Know your starting point. Made for the supported Windows Steam build. Check compatibility before installing. Multiplayer compatibility is not confirmed. Read the current compatibility notes ↗ "}, {"title": "Create your first mod", "href": "quickstart.html", "text": " # Create your first mod Put a greeting in the game. Change it. Reload it. Use the portable helper and **Hello MT2**. You need the supported Windows Steam game and a text editor. Lua needs no compiler. Download the [developer SDK](https://github.com/DooDesch-Mods/MMORPGTycoon2-ModFramework/releases), or follow the [contributor build guide](contributing/build.md) to build it. ## Install into your profile Download **MT2Framework-SDK** and extract it outside the game folder. Open `mt2fw/MT2FW-Helper.exe` in the SDK. The SDK includes the player runtime under `runtime/`. Select your Steam game and a mod profile, then choose **Install framework**. Keep the helper package outside the game directory so it remains available for removal. Unknown existing loaders are preserved and reported. ![The portable helper with installation and profile controls](public/media/helper-install.png) The capture shows the real helper. The profile gives your mods their own settings and game data. Existing multiplayer test profiles are not moved. Import a save only through the explicit copy action. ## Create Hello MT2 Select **Create a mod**, choose **Hello MT2**, and enter `yourname.hello`. Select your profile's `mods` folder as the parent. The helper creates the manifest, script, editor types and agent guide. ![The helper's mod creation controls](public/media/helper-create.png) ## Open the game Start through Steam. The **Hello MT2** window appears. Load a world to see its name. Open **Mods** on the game's desktop, or press F10. ![The native Mods app, with the selected mod and reload controls](public/media/mods-menu.png) ![The first Lua mod visible in the actual game](public/media/first-mod.png) ## Make it yours Open the generated `main.lua` in your text editor. Replace its first line with: ```lua local message = \"My first MT2 mod!\" ``` Save the file. Keep the rest of the generated script. ## Reload and see the change In **Mods**, select your mod and choose **Reload Lua**. Close Mods with Escape or F10 to see the new greeting without restarting the game. ![A changed greeting after reloading in the same running game](public/media/edited-mod.png) If replacement code has a syntax or activation error, the previous instance stays active. Read the displayed file, line and original cause, fix the script, then reload again. Other mods keep running. Content packages and native DLLs need restart. ## Check and share Choose **Check mod**, then **Pack mod ZIP**. Save the ZIP outside the mod source folder. Another player installs it with **Install mod ZIP**. ```powershell mt2fw check C:/Mods/yourname.hello mt2fw pack C:/Mods/yourname.hello C:/Mods/hello-1.0.0.zip ``` Include your own source, assets and license. Generated template code is MIT licensed; retain its notice. Choose a license for your original additions. Continue with [settings and storage](guides/config-storage.md), [API reference](reference/index.md) or [agent instructions](agents/start.md). 🛟 **Need help or found a bug?** Get support at [support.doodesch.de/modframework](https://support.doodesch.de/modframework). "}, {"title": "Capabilities", "href": "reference/capabilities.html", "text": " # Capabilities Capabilities belong in `mod.json`. Local mods cannot declare `objects.write`, `time.write` or `content`. Shared writes remain unavailable until the multiplayer bridge is accepted. ## ui - [`mt2.ui.window`](functions/ui-window.md) - [`mt2.ui.text`](functions/ui-text.md) - [`mt2.ui.button`](functions/ui-button.md) - [`mt2.ui.checkbox`](functions/ui-checkbox.md) - [`mt2.ui.number`](functions/ui-number.md) ## input - [`mt2.input.bind`](functions/input-bind.md) ## world.read - [`mt2.world.status`](functions/world-status.md) - [`mt2.time.get`](functions/time-get.md) ## objects.read - [`mt2.objects.list`](functions/objects-list.md) - [`mt2.objects.get`](functions/objects-get.md) - [`mt2.objects.selected`](functions/objects-selected.md) - [`mt2.objects.persist`](functions/objects-persist.md) - [`mt2.objects.resolve`](functions/objects-resolve.md) ## objects.write - [`mt2.actions.place`](functions/actions-place.md) - [`mt2.actions.move`](functions/actions-move.md) - [`mt2.actions.remove`](functions/actions-remove.md) - [`mt2.actions.status`](functions/actions-status.md) ## camera - [`mt2.camera.get`](functions/camera-get.md) - [`mt2.camera.focus`](functions/camera-focus.md) ## time.write - [`mt2.time.set`](functions/time-set.md) ## content - [`mt2.content.register`](functions/content-register.md) "}, {"title": "API changes", "href": "reference/changes.html", "text": " # API changes Baseline: API 1.0.0. Generated comparison covers names, parameter types/default metadata and return signatures; semantic changes require an explicit migration note. ```json { \"api_version\": \"1.0.0\", \"added\": [ \"mt2.patches.add\", \"mt2.patches.remove\", \"mt2.patches.status\", \"mt2.patches.targets\" ], \"removed\": [], \"changed_signatures\": [] } ``` "}, {"title": "Command-line reference", "href": "reference/cli.html", "text": " # Command-line reference The helper opens when no command is given. These options are generated from the same argparse parser used by the CLI. ```text usage: mt2fw [-h] {new,check,pack,doctor,install,uninstall,add} ... positional arguments: {new,check,pack,doctor,install,uninstall,add} options: -h, --help show this help message and exit ``` ## new ```text usage: mt2fw new [-h] [--template {hello,inspector,scenery,native}] id directory positional arguments: id directory options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --template {hello,inspector,scenery,native} ``` ## check ```text usage: mt2fw check [-h] directory positional arguments: directory options: -h, --help show this help message and exit ``` ## pack ```text usage: mt2fw pack [-h] directory output positional arguments: directory output options: -h, --help show this help message and exit ``` ## doctor ```text usage: mt2fw doctor [-h] [--game GAME] [--profile PROFILE] options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --game GAME --profile PROFILE ``` ## install ```text usage: mt2fw install [-h] [--profile PROFILE] package game positional arguments: package game options: -h, --help show this help message and exit --profile PROFILE ``` ## uninstall ```text usage: mt2fw uninstall [-h] game positional arguments: game options: -h, --help show this help message and exit ``` ## add ```text usage: mt2fw add [-h] archive profile positional arguments: archive profile options: -h, --help show this help message and exit ``` `new` creates a complete project. `check` validates it without executing scripts. `pack` writes an archive outside its source. `doctor` reads game/profile diagnostics. Installation commands preserve original game data and require the target game to be closed. "}, {"title": "Events", "href": "reference/events.html", "text": " # Events Subscribe with `mt2.events.on`. Callback errors retain their cause and disable the owner. A failed staged replacement keeps the previous instance. ## start Runs during preparation; game-dependent operations are unavailable. Payload: `table`. Since 1.0.0. ## stop Final callback before registrations are removed. Payload: `table`. Since 1.0.0. ## world_ready A world becomes ready, including activation into an existing world. Payload: `MT2WorldEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## world_leave The world is leaving; old handles expire. Payload: `MT2WorldEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## saved The original game save is confirmed and associated metadata is committed. Payload: `MT2SavedEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## update One game update with delta seconds. Payload: `MT2UpdateEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## render Draw UI widgets during this render callback. Payload: `table`. Since 1.0.0. ## input Owned hotkey event, suspended while the Mods menu is open. Payload: `MT2InputEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## object_changed A supported original native placement, move or removal was observed. Payload: `MT2ObjectChangedEvent`. Since 1.0.0. ## action_completed A queued world action has a terminal status; unknown is not success. Payload: `MT2Action`. Since 1.0.0. "}, {"title": "mt2.actions.move", "href": "reference/functions/actions-move.html", "text": " # mt2.actions.move Queue a checked native move. ```lua mt2.actions.move(handle: MT2Handle, x: number, y: number, z: number): MT2Action ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `handle` | `MT2Handle` | `Required` | Current object handle. | | `x` | `number` | `Required` | Target X. | | `y` | `number` | `Required` | Target Y; native settling may adjust ground height. | | `z` | `number` | `Required` | Target Z. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Action - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Queues a world mutation for the game thread; maximum 64 pending requests. World changes survive Lua reload. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Successful world effects are stored by the game when saved. - **Capabilities:** `objects.write` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - A loaded world, objects.write and singleplayer/shared class are required. - Only confirmed establishes success. Unknown must never be retried automatically. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/actions/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local pending, result mt2.config.define(\"x\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable X\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.config.define(\"z\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable Z\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.ui.window(\"Placement\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Choose an owned clear position in settings before placing.\") if mt2.world.status().loaded and mt2.ui.button(\"Queue scenery placement\") then pending = mt2.actions.place(\"mt2:Scenery/circustent\", mt2.config.get(\"x\"), 0, mt2.config.get(\"z\")) result = nil end if result then mt2.ui.text(result.status .. \": \" .. (result.cause or \"\")) end end) mt2.events.on(\"action_completed\", function(value) if pending and value.id == pending.id then result = value if value.status ~= \"confirmed\" then mt2.log.warn(value.cause or value.status) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.actions.place", "href": "reference/functions/actions-place.html", "text": " # mt2.actions.place Queue a checked native building placement. ```lua mt2.actions.place(definition: string, x: number, y: number, z: number): MT2Action ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `definition` | `string` | `Required` | Registered mod:id or mt2:Type/Variant. | | `x` | `number` | `Required` | World X. | | `y` | `number` | `Required` | Finite argument; native placement resolves terrain height. | | `z` | `number` | `Required` | World Z. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Action - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Queues a world mutation for the game thread; maximum 64 pending requests. World changes survive Lua reload. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Successful world effects are stored by the game when saved. - **Capabilities:** `objects.write` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - A loaded world, objects.write and singleplayer/shared class are required. - Only confirmed establishes success. Unknown must never be retried automatically. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/actions/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local pending, result mt2.config.define(\"x\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable X\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.config.define(\"z\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable Z\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.ui.window(\"Placement\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Choose an owned clear position in settings before placing.\") if mt2.world.status().loaded and mt2.ui.button(\"Queue scenery placement\") then pending = mt2.actions.place(\"mt2:Scenery/circustent\", mt2.config.get(\"x\"), 0, mt2.config.get(\"z\")) result = nil end if result then mt2.ui.text(result.status .. \": \" .. (result.cause or \"\")) end end) mt2.events.on(\"action_completed\", function(value) if pending and value.id == pending.id then result = value if value.status ~= \"confirmed\" then mt2.log.warn(value.cause or value.status) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.actions.remove", "href": "reference/functions/actions-remove.html", "text": " # mt2.actions.remove Queue a checked native removal. ```lua mt2.actions.remove(handle: MT2Handle): MT2Action ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `handle` | `MT2Handle` | `Required` | Current object handle. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Action - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Queues a world mutation for the game thread; maximum 64 pending requests. World changes survive Lua reload. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Successful world effects are stored by the game when saved. - **Capabilities:** `objects.write` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - A loaded world, objects.write and singleplayer/shared class are required. - Only confirmed establishes success. Unknown must never be retried automatically. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/actions/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local pending, result mt2.config.define(\"x\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable X\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.config.define(\"z\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable Z\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.ui.window(\"Placement\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Choose an owned clear position in settings before placing.\") if mt2.world.status().loaded and mt2.ui.button(\"Queue scenery placement\") then pending = mt2.actions.place(\"mt2:Scenery/circustent\", mt2.config.get(\"x\"), 0, mt2.config.get(\"z\")) result = nil end if result then mt2.ui.text(result.status .. \": \" .. (result.cause or \"\")) end end) mt2.events.on(\"action_completed\", function(value) if pending and value.id == pending.id then result = value if value.status ~= \"confirmed\" then mt2.log.warn(value.cause or value.status) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.actions.status", "href": "reference/functions/actions-status.html", "text": " # mt2.actions.status Read accepted, confirmed, rejected or unknown status. ```lua mt2.actions.status(id: string): MT2Action ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | `Required` | Action ID owned by this mod. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Action - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `objects.write` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - An unknown, expired or foreign action ID is rejected. - Only the last 256 completed requests are retained. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/actions/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local pending, result mt2.config.define(\"x\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable X\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.config.define(\"z\", {type=\"number\", default=0, label=\"Owned buildable Z\", min=-100000, max=100000}) mt2.ui.window(\"Placement\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Choose an owned clear position in settings before placing.\") if mt2.world.status().loaded and mt2.ui.button(\"Queue scenery placement\") then pending = mt2.actions.place(\"mt2:Scenery/circustent\", mt2.config.get(\"x\"), 0, mt2.config.get(\"z\")) result = nil end if result then mt2.ui.text(result.status .. \": \" .. (result.cause or \"\")) end end) mt2.events.on(\"action_completed\", function(value) if pending and value.id == pending.id then result = value if value.status ~= \"confirmed\" then mt2.log.warn(value.cause or value.status) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.camera.focus", "href": "reference/functions/camera-focus.html", "text": " # mt2.camera.focus Request the native edit camera to focus a position. ```lua mt2.camera.focus(x: number, y: number, z: number, distance: number): MT2Accepted ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `x` | `number` | `Required` | Focus X. | | `y` | `number` | `Required` | Focus height. | | `z` | `number` | `Required` | Focus Z. | | `distance` | `number` | `Required` | Native allowed zoom distance. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Accepted - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Moves the local camera; does not change world objects. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `camera` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Non-finite/out-of-range values, unavailable camera and a native camera script still controlling the view are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/camera/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Camera\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end local view = mt2.camera.get() mt2.ui.text(\"Distance: \" .. view.distance) if view.editable and mt2.ui.button(\"Focus here\") then mt2.camera.focus(view.focus_x, view.focus_y, view.focus_z, 100) end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.camera.get", "href": "reference/functions/camera-get.html", "text": " # mt2.camera.get Read camera position, focus and edit state. ```lua mt2.camera.get(): MT2Camera ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Camera - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `camera` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/camera/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Camera\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end local view = mt2.camera.get() mt2.ui.text(\"Distance: \" .. view.distance) if view.editable and mt2.ui.button(\"Focus here\") then mt2.camera.focus(view.focus_x, view.focus_y, view.focus_z, 100) end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.config.define", "href": "reference/functions/config-define.html", "text": " # mt2.config.define Define a typed setting shown in the Mods menu. ```lua mt2.config.define(key: string, schema: MT2Setting): boolean|string|number ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `key` | `string` | `Required` | Setting key. | | `schema` | `MT2Setting` | `Required` | Typed default, label and optional bounds. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean|string|number - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Registers a schema and validates an existing profile value. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Settings are scoped to mod and profile. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/config/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.config.define(\"greeting\", {type=\"string\", default=\"Hello, MT2!\", label=\"Greeting\"}) mt2.ui.window(\"Settings\", function() mt2.ui.text(mt2.config.get(\"greeting\")) if mt2.ui.button(\"Use a new greeting\") then mt2.config.set(\"greeting\", \"I made this.\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.config.get", "href": "reference/functions/config-get.html", "text": " # mt2.config.get Read a defined setting. ```lua mt2.config.get(key: string): boolean|string|number ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `key` | `string` | `Required` | Previously defined setting. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean|string|number - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - An undefined setting is rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/config/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.config.define(\"greeting\", {type=\"string\", default=\"Hello, MT2!\", label=\"Greeting\"}) mt2.ui.window(\"Settings\", function() mt2.ui.text(mt2.config.get(\"greeting\")) if mt2.ui.button(\"Use a new greeting\") then mt2.config.set(\"greeting\", \"I made this.\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.config.set", "href": "reference/functions/config-set.html", "text": " # mt2.config.set Validate and save a setting. ```lua mt2.config.set(key: string, value: boolean|string|number): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `key` | `string` | `Required` | Previously defined setting. | | `value` | `boolean\\|string\\|number` | `Required` | Value matching the declared schema. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Writes profile settings after successful activation. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Scoped to mod and profile. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Undefined keys, wrong types and values outside bounds are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/config/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.config.define(\"greeting\", {type=\"string\", default=\"Hello, MT2!\", label=\"Greeting\"}) mt2.ui.window(\"Settings\", function() mt2.ui.text(mt2.config.get(\"greeting\")) if mt2.ui.button(\"Use a new greeting\") then mt2.config.set(\"greeting\", \"I made this.\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.content.register", "href": "reference/functions/content-register.html", "text": " # mt2.content.register Register static scenery resources at startup. ```lua mt2.content.register(definitions: MT2ContentDefinition[]): MT2ContentReceipt ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `definitions` | `MT2ContentDefinition[]` | `Required` | Nonempty mod-namespaced content definitions. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2ContentReceipt - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation/start only, before a world is loaded. - **Effects:** Stages definitions then mounts their assets on activation. Content changes require restart. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Save metadata records required content package versions and hashes. - **Capabilities:** `content` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Duplicate IDs/resources, foreign namespaces, missing model/material files and late registration are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/content/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.content.register({{id=\"example.content:pyramid\",name=\"Pyramid\",variant=\"mt2fw_example_content_266054d085dd_pyramid\",model=\"assets/mt2fw/example_content_266054d085dd_pyramid/model.vmb\",material=\"assets/materials/mt2fw_example_content_266054d085dd_pyramid_0.mat\"}}) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.events.off", "href": "reference/functions/events-off.html", "text": " # mt2.events.off Remove your subscription. ```lua mt2.events.off(subscription: integer): nil ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `subscription` | `integer` | `Required` | The token returned by on, input.bind or ui.window. | ## Contract - **Returns:** nil - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Unsubscribes the token if owned by this mod. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/events/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function(event) mt2.log.info(\"World ready: \" .. event.name) end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function(event) mt2.log.info(\"Leaving: \" .. event.name) end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.events.on", "href": "reference/functions/events-on.html", "text": " # mt2.events.on Subscribe to an event. ```lua mt2.events.on(event: MT2EventName, callback: fun(event:MT2EventPayload)): integer ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `event` | `MT2EventName` | `Required` | One supported event name. | | `callback` | `fun(event:MT2EventPayload)` | `Required` | Receives the event payload. | ## Contract - **Returns:** integer - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Registers one mod-owned callback; maximum 2048 subscriptions per mod. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Subscription IDs are session-local. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/events/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function(event) mt2.log.info(\"World ready: \" .. event.name) end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function(event) mt2.log.info(\"Leaving: \" .. event.name) end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.input.bind", "href": "reference/functions/input-bind.html", "text": " # mt2.input.bind Bind an owned keyboard shortcut. ```lua mt2.input.bind(name: string, scancode: integer, callback: fun(event:MT2InputEvent)): integer ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `name` | `string` | `Required` | Mod-local shortcut name. | | `scancode` | `integer` | `Required` | SDL scancode, 1..511 excluding F10 (67). | | `callback` | `fun(event:MT2InputEvent)` | `Required` | Called for this mod and binding only. | ## Contract - **Returns:** integer - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Reserves the scancode and registers an input subscription. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `input` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - The input capability is required. - Invalid/reserved scancodes and shortcuts owned by another mod are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/input/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.input.bind(\"show_message\", 66, function(event) mt2.log.info(\"My F9 shortcut: \" .. event.name) end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.log.debug", "href": "reference/functions/log-debug.html", "text": " # mt2.log.debug Write a mod-labelled debug log entry. ```lua mt2.log.debug(message: any): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `message` | `any` | `Required` | Converted with tostring. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Appends to the bounded structured log. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/log/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.log.debug(\"Debug detail\") mt2.log.info(\"Mod prepared\") mt2.log.warn(\"Example warning\") mt2.log.error(\"Example error log; logging alone does not disable a mod\") ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.log.error", "href": "reference/functions/log-error.html", "text": " # mt2.log.error Write a mod-labelled error log entry. ```lua mt2.log.error(message: any): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `message` | `any` | `Required` | Converted with tostring. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Appends to the bounded structured log. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/log/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.log.debug(\"Debug detail\") mt2.log.info(\"Mod prepared\") mt2.log.warn(\"Example warning\") mt2.log.error(\"Example error log; logging alone does not disable a mod\") ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.log.info", "href": "reference/functions/log-info.html", "text": " # mt2.log.info Write a mod-labelled info log entry. ```lua mt2.log.info(message: any): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `message` | `any` | `Required` | Converted with tostring. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Appends to the bounded structured log. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/log/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.log.debug(\"Debug detail\") mt2.log.info(\"Mod prepared\") mt2.log.warn(\"Example warning\") mt2.log.error(\"Example error log; logging alone does not disable a mod\") ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.log.warn", "href": "reference/functions/log-warn.html", "text": " # mt2.log.warn Write a mod-labelled warn log entry. ```lua mt2.log.warn(message: any): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `message` | `any` | `Required` | Converted with tostring. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Appends to the bounded structured log. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/log/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.log.debug(\"Debug detail\") mt2.log.info(\"Mod prepared\") mt2.log.warn(\"Example warning\") mt2.log.error(\"Example error log; logging alone does not disable a mod\") ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.objects.get", "href": "reference/functions/objects-get.html", "text": " # mt2.objects.get Read a supported object. ```lua mt2.objects.get(handle: MT2Handle): MT2Object ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `handle` | `MT2Handle` | `Required` | Handle from this active world epoch. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Object - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `objects.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Missing world, wrong thread, expired epoch and deleted/unreadable objects are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/objects/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Selected object\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end if mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) local ok, id = pcall(mt2.objects.persist, selected.handle) if ok then mt2.storage.set(\"selected_building\", id, \"world\") else mt2.log.warn(\"This selection cannot be persisted: \" .. id) end end end end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local id = mt2.storage.get(\"selected_building\", nil, \"world\") if id then local ok, object = pcall(mt2.objects.resolve, id) if ok then mt2.log.info(\"Restored building: \" .. object.handle.uid) else mt2.log.warn(\"Saved reference unavailable: \" .. object) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.objects.list", "href": "reference/functions/objects-list.html", "text": " # mt2.objects.list Read a bounded list of supported props. ```lua mt2.objects.list(limit?: integer): MT2Object[] ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `limit` | `integer` | `100` | Maximum result count, 1..128. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Object[] - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Explicit bounded query; do not poll or scan the complete world each frame. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `objects.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/objects/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Selected object\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end if mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) local ok, id = pcall(mt2.objects.persist, selected.handle) if ok then mt2.storage.set(\"selected_building\", id, \"world\") else mt2.log.warn(\"This selection cannot be persisted: \" .. id) end end end end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local id = mt2.storage.get(\"selected_building\", nil, \"world\") if id then local ok, object = pcall(mt2.objects.resolve, id) if ok then mt2.log.info(\"Restored building: \" .. object.handle.uid) else mt2.log.warn(\"Saved reference unavailable: \" .. object) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.objects.persist", "href": "reference/functions/objects-persist.html", "text": " # mt2.objects.persist Create a mod-owned persistent building reference. ```lua mt2.objects.persist(handle: MT2Handle): string ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `handle` | `MT2Handle` | `Required` | A supported root-world building. | ## Contract - **Returns:** string - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Registers a persistent reference, maximum 1024 per world. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Retained only with a confirmed save; store the returned ID in world storage. - **Capabilities:** `objects.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Non-building/dungeon targets, expired handles and the reference budget are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/objects/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Selected object\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end if mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) local ok, id = pcall(mt2.objects.persist, selected.handle) if ok then mt2.storage.set(\"selected_building\", id, \"world\") else mt2.log.warn(\"This selection cannot be persisted: \" .. id) end end end end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local id = mt2.storage.get(\"selected_building\", nil, \"world\") if id then local ok, object = pcall(mt2.objects.resolve, id) if ok then mt2.log.info(\"Restored building: \" .. object.handle.uid) else mt2.log.warn(\"Saved reference unavailable: \" .. object) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.objects.resolve", "href": "reference/functions/objects-resolve.html", "text": " # mt2.objects.resolve Resolve a saved reference to a fresh session handle. ```lua mt2.objects.resolve(id: string): MT2Object ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | `Required` | A saved persistent reference owned by this mod. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Object - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** Checks saved region/building identity, type, variant and pose before rebinding. - **Capabilities:** `objects.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Missing, removed, mismatched or foreign references are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/objects/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Selected object\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end if mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) local ok, id = pcall(mt2.objects.persist, selected.handle) if ok then mt2.storage.set(\"selected_building\", id, \"world\") else mt2.log.warn(\"This selection cannot be persisted: \" .. id) end end end end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local id = mt2.storage.get(\"selected_building\", nil, \"world\") if id then local ok, object = pcall(mt2.objects.resolve, id) if ok then mt2.log.info(\"Restored building: \" .. object.handle.uid) else mt2.log.warn(\"Saved reference unavailable: \" .. object) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.objects.selected", "href": "reference/functions/objects-selected.html", "text": " # mt2.objects.selected Read the first supported selected object, or nil. ```lua mt2.objects.selected(): MT2Object|nil ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Object|nil - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `objects.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/objects/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Selected object\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end if mt2.ui.button(\"Read selection\") then local selected = mt2.objects.selected() if selected then mt2.log.info(\"UID: \" .. selected.handle.uid) local ok, id = pcall(mt2.objects.persist, selected.handle) if ok then mt2.storage.set(\"selected_building\", id, \"world\") else mt2.log.warn(\"This selection cannot be persisted: \" .. id) end end end end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local id = mt2.storage.get(\"selected_building\", nil, \"world\") if id then local ok, object = pcall(mt2.objects.resolve, id) if ok then mt2.log.info(\"Restored building: \" .. object.handle.uid) else mt2.log.warn(\"Saved reference unavailable: \" .. object) end end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.patches.add", "href": "reference/functions/patches-add.html", "text": " # mt2.patches.add Register an owned experimental Before/After patch on a named target. ```lua mt2.patches.add(spec: MT2PatchSpec, callbacks: MT2PatchCallbacks): string ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `spec` | `MT2PatchSpec` | `Required` | Mod-local ID, named target, mode and optional ordering. References use manifest-id:patch-id. | | `callbacks` | `MT2PatchCallbacks` | `Required` | At least one callback. Observe returns nil; modify Before may return typed replacement fields. | ## Contract - **Returns:** string - **Thread:** Framework game-thread callback; Lua skips native worker invocations. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Stages during preparation, otherwise atomically publishes a validated order. Observe requires world.read; api.time.set modify requires time.write. - **Reload:** A successful Lua replacement exchanges the complete owner set at frame end. A rejected candidate retains the previous instance and order. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** experimental-patches-1 ## Errors - Invalid target, unsupported mode, ordering cycle, cross-target reference or duplicate ID rejects registration. - A second api.time.set writer rejects with PATCH_WRITER_CONFLICT; previous generation remains active. - Patch callbacks cannot issue world-write API calls; modifiers return typed values. Unknown or Client authority rejects time mutation. Requires Framework **0.2.0** or newer, experimental **patches protocol 1**. See the [target contract](../../public/api/experimental/patches.json). ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/patch-observer/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local entered, returned, available = 0, 0, 0 local last_invocation = \"None\" local ready = false mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() ready = true end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function() ready = false end) mt2.patches.add({ id = \"count\", target = \"objects.create\", mode = \"observe\" }, { before = function(call) if not ready then return end entered = entered + 1 last_invocation = call.invocation end, after = function(call) if not ready then return end if call.original_returned then returned = returned + 1 end if call.result_available then available = available + 1 end end }) mt2.ui.window(\"Native create observer\", function() if not ready then mt2.ui.text(\"Load a world, then create an object.\") end mt2.ui.text(\"Native create calls entered: \" .. entered) mt2.ui.text(\"Original calls returned: \" .. returned) mt2.ui.text(\"Results available: \" .. available) mt2.ui.text(\"Last invocation: \" .. last_invocation) mt2.ui.text(\"Includes non-scenery creates.\") end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.patches.remove", "href": "reference/functions/patches-remove.html", "text": " # mt2.patches.remove Remove an owned patch and return whether its owner has no in-flight callbacks. ```lua mt2.patches.remove(id: string): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | `Required` | Mod-local patch ID owned by this instance. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework game-thread callback; Lua skips native worker invocations. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Removes future entries. An already entered snapshot completes in reverse order. - **Reload:** A successful Lua replacement exchanges the complete owner set at frame end. A rejected candidate retains the previous instance and order. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** experimental-patches-1 ## Errors - An unknown or foreign ID raises PATCH_NOT_FOUND. Requires Framework **0.2.0** or newer, experimental **patches protocol 1**. See the [target contract](../../public/api/experimental/patches.json). ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/patch-observer/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local entered, returned, available = 0, 0, 0 local last_invocation = \"None\" local ready = false mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() ready = true end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function() ready = false end) mt2.patches.add({ id = \"count\", target = \"objects.create\", mode = \"observe\" }, { before = function(call) if not ready then return end entered = entered + 1 last_invocation = call.invocation end, after = function(call) if not ready then return end if call.original_returned then returned = returned + 1 end if call.result_available then available = available + 1 end end }) mt2.ui.window(\"Native create observer\", function() if not ready then mt2.ui.text(\"Load a world, then create an object.\") end mt2.ui.text(\"Native create calls entered: \" .. entered) mt2.ui.text(\"Original calls returned: \" .. returned) mt2.ui.text(\"Results available: \" .. available) mt2.ui.text(\"Last invocation: \" .. last_invocation) mt2.ui.text(\"Includes non-scenery creates.\") end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.patches.status", "href": "reference/functions/patches-status.html", "text": " # mt2.patches.status Read published patch order, diagnostics and current time-write authority. ```lua mt2.patches.status(): MT2PatchStatus ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2PatchStatus - **Thread:** Framework game-thread callback; Lua skips native worker invocations. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Reads current order and asks the trusted native session provider for authority. It does not execute a world action. - **Reload:** A successful Lua replacement exchanges the complete owner set at frame end. A rejected candidate retains the previous instance and order. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** experimental-patches-1 ## Errors - A failed authority check remains visible in authority[\"api.time.set\"].reason. Requires Framework **0.2.0** or newer, experimental **patches protocol 1**. See the [target contract](../../public/api/experimental/patches.json). ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/time-policy/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local maximum_speed = 2 local message = \"Choose a request after loading a world.\" local last_warning local ready = false mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() ready = true end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function() ready = false end) mt2.patches.add({ id = \"limit-speed\", target = \"api.time.set\", mode = \"modify\" }, { before = function(call) return {speed = math.min(call.speed, maximum_speed)} end }) local function request_time(paused, speed) -- A denied authority check is expected here and must remain visible. local ok, result = pcall(mt2.time.set, paused, speed) if ok then message = \"Confirmed API clock: speed \" .. result.speed last_warning = nil else message = \"Blocked: \" .. tostring(result) if message ~= last_warning then mt2.log.warn(message) last_warning = message end end end mt2.ui.window(\"API time policy\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Framework time.set speed limit: \" .. maximum_speed) mt2.ui.text(\"Native game controls keep their behavior.\") local authority = mt2.patches.status().authority[\"api.time.set\"] if not authority.allowed then mt2.ui.text(\"Blocked: \" .. authority.reason) end if ready then if mt2.ui.button(\"Request speed 4 (limit 2)\") then request_time(false, 4) end if mt2.ui.button(\"Request pause\") then request_time(true, 1) end else mt2.ui.text(\"Load a world to send a time request.\") end mt2.ui.text(message) end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.patches.targets", "href": "reference/functions/patches-targets.html", "text": " # mt2.patches.targets Discover supported experimental patch targets, phases and modes. ```lua mt2.patches.targets(): MT2PatchTarget[] ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2PatchTarget[] - **Thread:** Framework game-thread callback; Lua skips native worker invocations. - **When to call:** Script preparation or an active callback. - **Effects:** Reads target metadata without changing registrations or the world. - **Reload:** A successful Lua replacement exchanges the complete owner set at frame end. A rejected candidate retains the previous instance and order. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** experimental-patches-1 ## Errors - Invalid host arguments raise a Lua error. Requires Framework **0.2.0** or newer, experimental **patches protocol 1**. See the [target contract](../../public/api/experimental/patches.json). ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/patch-observer/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local entered, returned, available = 0, 0, 0 local last_invocation = \"None\" local ready = false mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() ready = true end) mt2.events.on(\"world_leave\", function() ready = false end) mt2.patches.add({ id = \"count\", target = \"objects.create\", mode = \"observe\" }, { before = function(call) if not ready then return end entered = entered + 1 last_invocation = call.invocation end, after = function(call) if not ready then return end if call.original_returned then returned = returned + 1 end if call.result_available then available = available + 1 end end }) mt2.ui.window(\"Native create observer\", function() if not ready then mt2.ui.text(\"Load a world, then create an object.\") end mt2.ui.text(\"Native create calls entered: \" .. entered) mt2.ui.text(\"Original calls returned: \" .. returned) mt2.ui.text(\"Results available: \" .. available) mt2.ui.text(\"Last invocation: \" .. last_invocation) mt2.ui.text(\"Includes non-scenery creates.\") end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.storage.get", "href": "reference/functions/storage-get.html", "text": " # mt2.storage.get Read mod-owned profile or world data. ```lua mt2.storage.get(key: string, default?: any, scope?: 'profile'|'world'): any ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `key` | `string` | `Required` | Mod-local key. | | `default` | `any` | `nil` | Fallback for a missing key. | | `scope` | `'profile'\\|'world'` | `\"profile\"` | Storage scope. | ## Contract - **Returns:** any - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Preparation/callbacks for profile data; a loaded world for world data. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** World data restores from the exact confirmed save metadata. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/storage/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.events.on(\"start\", function() local starts = mt2.storage.get(\"starts\", 0) mt2.storage.set(\"starts\", starts + 1) end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local visits = mt2.storage.get(\"visits\", 0, \"world\") mt2.storage.set(\"visits\", visits + 1, \"world\") end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.storage.set", "href": "reference/functions/storage-set.html", "text": " # mt2.storage.set Store mod-owned profile or world data. ```lua mt2.storage.set(key: string, value: any, scope?: 'profile'|'world'): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `key` | `string` | `Required` | Mod-local key. | | `value` | `any` | `Required` | JSON-compatible data. | | `scope` | `'profile'\\|'world'` | `\"profile\"` | Storage scope. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Preparation/callbacks for profile data; a loaded world for world data. - **Effects:** Updates mod-owned data; profile data writes after activation, never as an implicit per-frame operation. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** World data is committed only with a confirmed game save. - **Capabilities:** None - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid scopes, cyclic/non-JSON data, missing world and data larger than 1 MiB are rejected. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/storage/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.events.on(\"start\", function() local starts = mt2.storage.get(\"starts\", 0) mt2.storage.set(\"starts\", starts + 1) end) mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local visits = mt2.storage.get(\"visits\", 0, \"world\") mt2.storage.set(\"visits\", visits + 1, \"world\") end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.time.get", "href": "reference/functions/time-get.html", "text": " # mt2.time.get Read pause, simulation speed and clock. ```lua mt2.time.get(): MT2Clock ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Clock - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `world.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/time/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Time\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end local clock = mt2.time.get() mt2.ui.text(\"Day: \" .. clock.day) if mt2.ui.button(\"Pause\") then mt2.time.set(true, 1) end if mt2.ui.button(\"Resume\") then mt2.time.set(false, 1) end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.time.set", "href": "reference/functions/time-set.html", "text": " # mt2.time.set Set pause and speed, then read them back. ```lua mt2.time.set(paused: boolean, speed?: number): MT2Clock ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `paused` | `boolean` | `Required` | Whether simulation is paused. | | `speed` | `number` | `1` | Native simulation speed. | ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2Clock - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Changes simulation time; local multiplayer class is not allowed. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `time.write` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid speed, unavailable world and shared writes without the multiplayer bridge are rejected. - Framework 0.2.0 requires acknowledged standalone or trusted Host authority; Unknown and Client never execute the request. - An invalid or failing experimental api.time.set writer rejects before the original request. Existing multiplayer bridge fences still apply. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/time/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.ui.window(\"Time\", function() if not mt2.world.status().loaded then return end local clock = mt2.time.get() mt2.ui.text(\"Day: \" .. clock.day) if mt2.ui.button(\"Pause\") then mt2.time.set(true, 1) end if mt2.ui.button(\"Resume\") then mt2.time.set(false, 1) end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.ui.button", "href": "reference/functions/ui-button.html", "text": " # mt2.ui.button Draw a button; true on the frame it is pressed. ```lua mt2.ui.button(label: string): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `label` | `string` | `Required` | Button label. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Only within ui.window or a render callback. - **Effects:** Draws a widget. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `ui` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/ui/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local enabled, amount = true, 5 mt2.ui.window(\"My mod\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Hello, MT2!\") enabled = mt2.ui.checkbox(\"Enabled\", enabled) amount = mt2.ui.number(\"Amount\", amount, 1, 10) if mt2.ui.button(\"Say hello\") then mt2.log.info(\"Hello from my mod\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.ui.checkbox", "href": "reference/functions/ui-checkbox.html", "text": " # mt2.ui.checkbox Draw a checkbox and return its current value. ```lua mt2.ui.checkbox(label: string, value: boolean): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `label` | `string` | `Required` | Checkbox label. | | `value` | `boolean` | `Required` | Current value. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Only within ui.window or a render callback. - **Effects:** Draws a widget; assign the returned value in your mod. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `ui` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/ui/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local enabled, amount = true, 5 mt2.ui.window(\"My mod\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Hello, MT2!\") enabled = mt2.ui.checkbox(\"Enabled\", enabled) amount = mt2.ui.number(\"Amount\", amount, 1, 10) if mt2.ui.button(\"Say hello\") then mt2.log.info(\"Hello from my mod\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.ui.number", "href": "reference/functions/ui-number.html", "text": " # mt2.ui.number Draw a numeric slider and return its value. ```lua mt2.ui.number(label: string, value: number, min: number, max: number): number ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `label` | `string` | `Required` | Slider label. | | `value` | `number` | `Required` | Current value. | | `min` | `number` | `Required` | Lower bound. | | `max` | `number` | `Required` | Upper bound. | ## Contract - **Returns:** number - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Only within ui.window or a render callback. - **Effects:** Draws a widget; assign the returned value in your mod. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `ui` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/ui/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local enabled, amount = true, 5 mt2.ui.window(\"My mod\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Hello, MT2!\") enabled = mt2.ui.checkbox(\"Enabled\", enabled) amount = mt2.ui.number(\"Amount\", amount, 1, 10) if mt2.ui.button(\"Say hello\") then mt2.log.info(\"Hello from my mod\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.ui.text", "href": "reference/functions/ui-text.html", "text": " # mt2.ui.text Draw text in the current window. ```lua mt2.ui.text(text: any): boolean ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `text` | `any` | `Required` | Converted to text, at most 8192 UTF-8 bytes per widget. | ## Contract - **Returns:** boolean - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Only within ui.window or a render callback. - **Effects:** Draws a widget. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `ui` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/ui/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local enabled, amount = true, 5 mt2.ui.window(\"My mod\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Hello, MT2!\") enabled = mt2.ui.checkbox(\"Enabled\", enabled) amount = mt2.ui.number(\"Amount\", amount, 1, 10) if mt2.ui.button(\"Say hello\") then mt2.log.info(\"Hello from my mod\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.ui.window", "href": "reference/functions/ui-window.html", "text": " # mt2.ui.window Register a window once and draw its contents on render. ```lua mt2.ui.window(title: string, draw: fun()): integer ``` ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default | Meaning | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | `title` | `string` | `Required` | Window title, at most 256 UTF-8 bytes. | | `draw` | `fun()` | `Required` | Draw widgets inside this callback. | ## Contract - **Returns:** integer - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Register during script preparation or a callback; draw runs only during render. - **Effects:** Registers a render subscription. Never register a window on every update. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `ui` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/ui/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT local enabled, amount = true, 5 mt2.ui.window(\"My mod\", function() mt2.ui.text(\"Hello, MT2!\") enabled = mt2.ui.checkbox(\"Enabled\", enabled) amount = mt2.ui.number(\"Amount\", amount, 1, 10) if mt2.ui.button(\"Say hello\") then mt2.log.info(\"Hello from my mod\") end end) ``` "}, {"title": "mt2.world.status", "href": "reference/functions/world-status.html", "text": " # mt2.world.status Read the loaded world name and current epoch. ```lua mt2.world.status(): MT2World ``` ## Contract - **Returns:** MT2World - **Thread:** Framework callbacks; native object/camera/time reads verify the game thread. - **When to call:** Active callbacks. Game-dependent calls reject during script preparation and start. - **Effects:** Reads state only. - **Reload:** Subscriptions owned by the old instance are removed on a successful replacement. - **Persistence:** No implicit persistence. - **Capabilities:** `world.read` - **Since:** 1.0.0 - **Stability:** stable-within-api-1 ## Errors - Invalid arguments raise a Lua error. - A required capability or unavailable adapter raises an error. ## Example This complete example is also available under `examples/world/main.lua` in the source and SDK documentation bundle. Native effects require the real game. ```lua -- SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT mt2.events.on(\"world_ready\", function() local world = mt2.world.status() mt2.log.info(world.name .. \" - epoch \" .. world.epoch) end) ``` "}, {"title": "API reference", "href": "reference/index.html", "text": " # API reference Public Lua API **1.0.0**, native ABI **1**, framework **0.2.0**. [Events](events.md) | [Types](types.md) | [Capabilities](capabilities.md) | [Native ABI](native.md) | [Manifests](manifests.md) ## actions - [`mt2.actions.place`](functions/actions-place.md): Queue a checked native building placement. - [`mt2.actions.move`](functions/actions-move.md): Queue a checked native move. - [`mt2.actions.remove`](functions/actions-remove.md): Queue a checked native removal. - [`mt2.actions.status`](functions/actions-status.md): Read accepted, confirmed, rejected or unknown status. ## camera - [`mt2.camera.get`](functions/camera-get.md): Read camera position, focus and edit state. - [`mt2.camera.focus`](functions/camera-focus.md): Request the native edit camera to focus a position. ## config - [`mt2.config.define`](functions/config-define.md): Define a typed setting shown in the Mods menu. - [`mt2.config.get`](functions/config-get.md): Read a defined setting. - [`mt2.config.set`](functions/config-set.md): Validate and save a setting. ## content - [`mt2.content.register`](functions/content-register.md): Register static scenery resources at startup. ## events - [`mt2.events.on`](functions/events-on.md): Subscribe to an event. - [`mt2.events.off`](functions/events-off.md): Remove your subscription. ## input - [`mt2.input.bind`](functions/input-bind.md): Bind an owned keyboard shortcut. ## log - [`mt2.log.debug`](functions/log-debug.md): Write a mod-labelled debug log entry. - [`mt2.log.info`](functions/log-info.md): Write a mod-labelled info log entry. - [`mt2.log.warn`](functions/log-warn.md): Write a mod-labelled warn log entry. - [`mt2.log.error`](functions/log-error.md): Write a mod-labelled error log entry. ## objects - [`mt2.objects.list`](functions/objects-list.md): Read a bounded list of supported props. - [`mt2.objects.get`](functions/objects-get.md): Read a supported object. - [`mt2.objects.selected`](functions/objects-selected.md): Read the first supported selected object, or nil. - [`mt2.objects.persist`](functions/objects-persist.md): Create a mod-owned persistent building reference. - [`mt2.objects.resolve`](functions/objects-resolve.md): Resolve a saved reference to a fresh session handle. ## patches - [`mt2.patches.add`](functions/patches-add.md): Register an owned experimental Before/After patch on a named target. - [`mt2.patches.remove`](functions/patches-remove.md): Remove an owned patch and return whether its owner has no in-flight callbacks. - [`mt2.patches.targets`](functions/patches-targets.md): Discover supported experimental patch targets, phases and modes. - [`mt2.patches.status`](functions/patches-status.md): Read published patch order, diagnostics and current time-write authority. ## storage - [`mt2.storage.get`](functions/storage-get.md): Read mod-owned profile or world data. - [`mt2.storage.set`](functions/storage-set.md): Store mod-owned profile or world data. ## time - [`mt2.time.get`](functions/time-get.md): Read pause, simulation speed and clock. - [`mt2.time.set`](functions/time-set.md): Set pause and speed, then read them back. ## ui - [`mt2.ui.window`](functions/ui-window.md): Register a window once and draw its contents on render. - [`mt2.ui.text`](functions/ui-text.md): Draw text in the current window. - [`mt2.ui.button`](functions/ui-button.md): Draw a button; true on the frame it is pressed. - [`mt2.ui.checkbox`](functions/ui-checkbox.md): Draw a checkbox and return its current value. - [`mt2.ui.number`](functions/ui-number.md): Draw a numeric slider and return its value. ## world - [`mt2.world.status`](functions/world-status.md): Read the loaded world name and current epoch. "}, {"title": "Mod and content manifests", "href": "reference/manifests.html", "text": " # Mod and content manifests Every package has one `mod.json` at its root. ```json { \"id\": \"yourname.hello\", \"name\": \"My first mod\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"api_version\": \"1.0.0\", \"type\": \"lua\", \"entry\": \"main.lua\", \"capabilities\": [\"ui\"], \"multiplayer\": \"local\" } ``` Required keys are ID, name, SemVer version, API version, type, entry and capabilities. IDs are lowercase and namespaced. Types are `lua`, `native` or `content`. Entry paths are confined to the package. Optional `dependencies` maps mod IDs to supported exact, `^` or `>=` version requirements. Missing dependencies, duplicate IDs and cycles receive concrete errors. The default multiplayer class is `singleplayer`. `local` is for UI, input and local camera, and cannot request world/content/time writes. `shared` requires matching packages and the future accepted multiplayer bridge; it does not enable shared writes in this candidate. Machine schemas: [mod.schema.json](../public/api/1.0.0/mod.schema.json) and [content.schema.json](../public/api/1.0.0/content.schema.json). They describe structure. `mt2fw check` additionally checks SemVer rules, Windows-confined paths, existing resources and package limits. Custom schema formats are `semver`, `supported-mt2-api` and `mt2-relative-path`; generic validators must register them or use the CLI for semantic validation. Content definitions require `id`, `variant`, `model` and `material`. Resource paths remain inside the package. Use the converter instead of editing binary files by hand. See [capabilities](capabilities.md) and [scenery](../guides/scenery.md). "}, {"title": "Native C ABI", "href": "reference/native.html", "text": " # Native C ABI The supported native boundary is `include/mt2fw/plugin.h`, shown below without a manually duplicated declaration list. No STL objects or exceptions may cross this ABI. Plugins remain loaded until process exit. Host strings are copied during a request. Callback JSON is borrowed until return. The host and PluginInfo strings remain valid through Shutdown. A size query never executes a mutation; request a sufficiently sized buffer once, then perform the call once. ```c #pragma once #include #include #ifdef _WIN32 #define MT2FW_EXPORT __declspec(dllexport) #define MT2FW_CALL __cdecl #else #define MT2FW_EXPORT __attribute__((visibility(\"default\"))) #define MT2FW_CALL #endif #ifdef __cplusplus extern \"C\" { #endif #define MT2FW_ABI_VERSION 1u typedef void (MT2FW_CALL *MT2FW_EventCallback)(void* user, const char* json, size_t length); typedef struct MT2FW_Host { uint32_t size; uint32_t abi_version; void* context; /* UTF-8 JSON. The returned required size includes the terminator. A null output only measures; it never executes a mutation. Call once with a sufficiently sized buffer. */ size_t (MT2FW_CALL *request)(void* context, const char* operation, const char* arguments, char* output, size_t capacity); uint64_t (MT2FW_CALL *subscribe)(void* context, const char* event, MT2FW_EventCallback callback, void* user); void (MT2FW_CALL *unsubscribe)(void* context, uint64_t subscription); } MT2FW_Host; typedef struct MT2FW_PluginInfo { uint32_t size; uint32_t abi_version; /* Borrowed UTF-8 strings; keep them valid until PluginShutdown returns. */ const char* id; const char* version; } MT2FW_PluginInfo; typedef int (MT2FW_CALL *MT2FW_QueryFn)(MT2FW_PluginInfo* info); typedef int (MT2FW_CALL *MT2FW_LoadFn)(const MT2FW_Host* host); typedef void (MT2FW_CALL *MT2FW_ShutdownFn)(void); /* Export MT2FW_PluginQuery, MT2FW_PluginLoad, MT2FW_PluginShutdown with these signatures. Strings passed to the host are copied during the call. Callback JSON is borrowed until return. The host remains valid until Shutdown returns. No exception may cross this boundary. */ #ifdef __cplusplus } #endif ``` ## Host JSON operations Lua wrappers use the following operations. `ui.begin` and `ui.end` are native host operations; Lua authors use `mt2.ui.window`. Events use the ABI subscribe/unsubscribe pointers. - `actions.move` - `actions.place` - `actions.remove` - `actions.status` - `camera.focus` - `camera.get` - `config.define` - `config.get` - `config.set` - `content.register` - `input.bind` - `log` - `objects.get` - `objects.list` - `objects.persist` - `objects.resolve` - `objects.selected` - `patches.status` - `patches.targets` - `storage.get` - `storage.set` - `time.get` - `time.set` - `ui.begin` - `ui.button` - `ui.checkbox` - `ui.end` - `ui.number` - `ui.text` - `world.status` "}, {"title": "Types", "href": "reference/types.html", "text": " # Types Handles carry a world epoch and an exact string UID. Never convert the UID to a floating-point Lua number. ## MT2Handle | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `epoch` | `integer` | | `uid` | `string` | ## MT2Object | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `handle` | `MT2Handle` | | `x` | `number` | | `y` | `number` | | `z` | `number` | | `deleted?` | `boolean` | ## MT2World | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `loaded` | `boolean` | | `name` | `string` | | `epoch` | `integer` | ## MT2Setting | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `type` | `'boolean'\\|'string'\\|'number'\\|'integer'` | | `default` | `boolean\\|string\\|number` | | `label?` | `string` | | `min?` | `number` | | `max?` | `number` | ## MT2Camera | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `x` | `number` | | `y` | `number` | | `z` | `number` | | `focus_x` | `number` | | `focus_y` | `number` | | `focus_z` | `number` | | `distance` | `number` | | `min` | `number` | | `max` | `number` | | `script_weight` | `number` | | `editable` | `boolean` | ## MT2Clock | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `paused` | `boolean` | | `speed` | `number` | | `day` | `integer` | | `seconds` | `integer` | ## MT2Accepted | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `accepted` | `boolean` | ## MT2Action | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | | `status` | `'accepted'\\|'confirmed'\\|'rejected'\\|'unknown'` | | `cause?` | `string` | | `native_attempted?` | `boolean` | | `object?` | `MT2Object` | ## MT2ContentDefinition | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | | `name?` | `string` | | `variant` | `string` | | `model` | `string` | | `material` | `string` | ## MT2ContentReceipt | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `staged?` | `integer` | | `registered?` | `integer` | ## MT2WorldEvent | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `epoch` | `integer` | | `name` | `string` | ## MT2SavedEvent | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `epoch` | `integer` | | `name` | `string` | | `save` | `string` | ## MT2UpdateEvent | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `dt` | `number` | | `epoch` | `integer` | ## MT2InputEvent | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `owner` | `string` | | `name` | `string` | | `scancode` | `integer` | ## MT2ObjectChangedEvent | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `operation` | `'actions.place'\\|'actions.move'\\|'actions.remove'` | | `source` | `'native'` | | `object` | `MT2Object` | ## MT2PatchSpec | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | | `target` | `'game.update'\\|'objects.create'\\|'api.time.set'` | | `mode?` | `'observe'\\|'modify'` | | `priority?` | `integer` | | `before?` | `string[]` | | `after?` | `string[]` | ## MT2PatchCall | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `target` | `string` | | `phase` | `'before'\\|'after'` | | `invocation` | `string` | | `generation` | `string` | | `epoch` | `string` | | `dt?` | `number` | | `thread?` | `string` | | `flag?` | `boolean` | | `paused?` | `boolean` | | `speed?` | `number` | | `original_returned?` | `boolean` | | `result_available?` | `boolean` | | `outcome?` | `'confirmed'\\|'rejected'\\|'unknown'` | | `result?` | `MT2Clock` | ## MT2PatchEdit | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `paused?` | `boolean` | | `speed?` | `number` | ## MT2PatchCallbacks | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `before?` | `fun(call:MT2PatchCall):MT2PatchEdit\\|nil` | | `after?` | `fun(call:MT2PatchCall)` | ## MT2PatchTarget | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | | `origin` | `'native'\\|'framework_request'` | | `phases` | `string[]` | | `modes` | `string[]` | | `write_slot?` | `string` | | `authority?` | `string` | | `lua_thread?` | `string` | ## MT2PatchRecord | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `id` | `string` | | `owner` | `string` | | `key` | `string` | | `target` | `string` | | `mode` | `string` | | `priority` | `integer` | | `order` | `integer` | | `unresolved` | `string[]` | | `last_error` | `string` | | `suppressed_errors` | `integer` | ## MT2PatchAuthority | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `allowed` | `boolean` | | `reason` | `string` | ## MT2PatchStatus | Field | Type | | --- | --- | | `generation` | `string` | | `targets` | `MT2PatchTarget[]` | | `patches` | `MT2PatchRecord[]` | | `authority` | `table ` | "}];
